WEBVTT

1
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:36.520
(♪ Rhythmic music playing)

2
00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:15.840
(Konstantin) Right, that's it.
Stop, stop.

3
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.400
(Arkadina) What's the matter?

4
00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:18.520
Stop!

5
00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.120
Oh, please don't get angry.

6
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:23.880
Look, I'll be quiet.
We'll all be quiet.

7
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.800
No, I forgot.

8
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.960
Only certain people
can write or perform on stage.

9
00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:30.400
And they must do it your way.

10
00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.000
The gate keepers
of creativity and culture,

11
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:35.520
and you only get to be one
if you copied whoever came before you.

12
00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.720
I'm sorry, I'm trying to break
the monopoly. Sorry to...

13
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:40.640
I'm sorry, Nina.

14
00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.240
We're not going on, are we?

15
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.240
Well, the playwright's run off.

16
00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:50.400
Creative differences with his audience.

17
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.440
- You've hurt his pride.
- No, I haven't.

18
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:57.480
- He can't possibly be that sensitive.
- He's embarrassed.

19
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:02.000
Well, he won't last very long as a writer
if he can't hear criticism.

20
00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:05.560
Isn't that why you show it
to people close to you first?

21
00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.800
I prefer to hear it
from those close to me.

22
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.360
I ask for it, in fact.

23
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.240
And I was watching.
We were all watching.

24
00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.760
We gave up our evening to come
all the way down here and watch a play,

25
00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:21.800
a play which he said
was just some silly thing

26
00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:23.520
he'd scribbled down for fun,

27
00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.680
and then there's a momentary lapse
in rapt, adoring attention.

28
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:31.040
I mean, what did he expect?

29
00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:34.640
For it to slowly dawn on me
that he'd written a masterpiece?

30
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:39.520
He chose his subject, as is his right,
and then he did his best.

31
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:41.680
Let him write, let him try.

32
00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.520
And I'm not trying stop him!

33
00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:46.920
I just ask

34
00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:51.160
that any explicit or implicit opinions
he has about my acting career

35
00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:52.640
be kept out of it.

36
00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:55.840
Come not between a dragon
and its wrath.

37
00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:57.800
I am not a dragon.

38
00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.520
I am a woman.

39
00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:02.720
As you well know.

40
00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:04.160
And there's no wrath.

41
00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.280
I'm just disappointed he chooses
to spend his time writing a play

42
00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:09.320
with the sole purpose of annoying me.

43
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:12.480
- Seems very pointless.
- That was not the sole purpose.

44
00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.440
Oh, you've defended him.
You have done your duty.

45
00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.880
- I understand whose side you're on.
- Come on, Irina. I'm on both your sides.

46
00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:21.040
He was trying to impress you.

47
00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:22.960
If you want to impress someone,

48
00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:26.320
you write something
you think they might like.

49
00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:30.360
He could have written something normal.

50
00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:33.520
And he wasn't trying to impress me,
he was trying to teach me,

51
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:35.920
show me what theatre should really be,
what real art is,

52
00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.160
because I'm some sell-out whore
and he's blazing some new artistic path.

53
00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.400
And I'm supposed
to sit and ignore him, Petrusha,

54
00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.920
deliberately provoking me,

55
00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:48.600
and then tell him how his first ever
bit of writing he's ever shown to anyone

56
00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.120
has changed theatre forever

57
00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:52.720
and that I've wasted
my entire fucking life.

58
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.360
It's beyond arrogant.

59
00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.320
The more I think about it,
the angrier I get, actually.

60
00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:01.640
Bravo, my dear.

61
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:04.400
Oh, sorry! Yes, well done!

62
00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:09.480
Oh, you were wonderful,
absolutely wonderful.

63
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:10.960
Why are you here, darling?

64
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:12.080
Hm?

65
00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.240
Messing around with Kostya

66
00:05:13.360 --> 00:05:15.840
and trying valiantly
to get a performance out of that.

67
00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:18.920
Why aren't you
making a real go of acting?

68
00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:21.880
You really do have something.
Don't you both think?

69
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:23.600
Don't you want to be a real actress?

70
00:05:23.720 --> 00:05:27.560
- Well, I'd love to, but...
- We have to take risks.

71
00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:30.320
And the bigger the risk...

72
00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:31.680
Don't they say?

73
00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.720
And you have such a pretty face.

74
00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:39.000
People with ten times your talent
would never get half the chances you will

75
00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.440
looking like that.

76
00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.040
You'd be in films, no trouble.

77
00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:46.160
Films?

78
00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:47.400
Oh, look at her.

79
00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:49.720
Yes, darling,
like they have in the cinema.

80
00:05:51.200 --> 00:05:54.800
Get Boris to give you a part.
They're always asking him his dream cast.

81
00:05:54.920 --> 00:05:55.880
Oh.

82
00:05:56.360 --> 00:05:59.720
Oh, sorry!
Kostya has quite thrown me off.

83
00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.560
Nina, this is Boris Trigorin,

84
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.840
writer extraordinaire

85
00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:07.600
and my sometime lover.

86
00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:13.440
And, Boris, here is Nina,
your new leading lady.

87
00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:17.440
It is amazing to meet you.

88
00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:19.600
I've read all your books. Twice.

89
00:06:19.760 --> 00:06:22.280
Oh, darling, don't get starstruck.

90
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.960
You will cripple him with embarrassment.

91
00:06:25.080 --> 00:06:28.120
I've never met anyone worse
at being famous than this man.

92
00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.720
Well, you were just in a play
he watched a good one third of.

93
00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:35.920
You have lots to talk about.

94
00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:37.560
You do not need me.

95
00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.320
It was just all speeches.

96
00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:41.480
No action.

97
00:06:41.640 --> 00:06:43.880
I always thought
good plays have a love story.

98
00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:49.440
It... It's a...
It was a, um, strange play.

99
00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:50.560
I think.

100
00:06:51.960 --> 00:06:53.600
Did you, sort of...?

101
00:06:57.720 --> 00:06:59.880
I didn't... I didn't really know
what was going on.

102
00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:02.600
Yes.

103
00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:05.080
I thought you were very sincere though.

104
00:07:05.200 --> 00:07:07.040
Truthful.

105
00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:08.840
And the lake, it must be full of fish.

106
00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.400
- What?
- Yes.

107
00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.800
It is, it is full.

108
00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:14.120
I like to fish.

109
00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:16.320
- You do?
- Yes, I like to fish.

110
00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.240
- Yes?
- There's nothing better.

111
00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:20.000
Well, surely after writing.

112
00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.240
Oh, and it was going so well!

113
00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:25.600
He sinks when you're nice to him.

114
00:07:25.720 --> 00:07:27.720
I went to the opera once.

115
00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.560
No, sinking,
they were talking about sinking,

116
00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:32.480
so I'm telling them something.

117
00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:34.560
So, I went to the opera and we'd...

118
00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:37.760
There was this singer.
What was his name? Big guy, Italian.

119
00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:39.360
- What was his name?
- I have no idea.

120
00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.440
Ah, he was famous.
Famous, like, opera singer.

121
00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:44.720
And this guy, whatever his name was,
this singer...

122
00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:46.280
Long hair, big guy.

123
00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.640
Yeah, this singer,
he sung, like, a what's it called?

124
00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.680
Like, a bottom C, low.

125
00:07:52.800 --> 00:07:55.200
Hit, like, a low note.

126
00:07:55.320 --> 00:07:56.640
And then, and then,

127
00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.040
one of the, what d'you call it, basses,

128
00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.640
the basses from our choir...

129
00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:03.560
I was there with the church choir.
It was, like, a church choir outing.

130
00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:06.520
We'd gone to see this guy,
the big guy that just hit bottom C,

131
00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:08.280
and he was a proper singer,

132
00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:10.400
like, a professional opera singer,

133
00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:13.440
and... and he'd done this low note.

134
00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:16.000
And then, and then,
one of the basses

135
00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:18.040
from our choir who we was with,

136
00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:22.680
he shouted up to the fat guy
who'd just hit this low note on stage,

137
00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:26.760
which was impressive,
impressive low note, and...

138
00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:29.640
But my guy,
he shouted out, "Bravo!"

139
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:31.360
But he shouted it out...

140
00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:33.880
lower!

141
00:08:34.920 --> 00:08:37.560
Like, a whole... What's it called?
Whole... octave.

142
00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:39.480
That's it. A whole octave lower.

143
00:08:39.560 --> 00:08:40.520
(Deep voice) "Bravo!"

144
00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:43.120
Like, up at him.
Like, at the professional.

145
00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:44.560
(Deep voice) "Bravo!"

146
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:46.000
It was...

147
00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:47.800
It was mad.

148
00:08:47.920 --> 00:08:48.880
(Laughs)

149
00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:51.680
It was... extraordinary.

150
00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:56.000
That's what they said.
I remember they said extraordinary.

151
00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:59.360
Fucking extraordinary!

152
00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:06.720
(♪ Faint piano playing)

153
00:09:06.840 --> 00:09:08.320
There's music coming from somewhere.

154
00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:10.480
It's from across the lake.

155
00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:12.680
(♪ Piano music continues)

156
00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:17.880
Ten, fifteen years ago there'd be singing
every night round here.

157
00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:21.760
Singing, noise,

158
00:09:21.880 --> 00:09:23.920
parties,

159
00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.800
affairs,

160
00:09:25.920 --> 00:09:27.640
all sorts.

161
00:09:27.760 --> 00:09:29.200
All sorts.

162
00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.520
There were two other
big estate houses like this

163
00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:34.360
and we'd be up all night,

164
00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.120
forget why people ever went to sleep,

165
00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:40.800
how they ever could.

166
00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:43.680
All sorts we got up to.

167
00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:48.400
Nothing else to do here, and most of us
were sensible enough not to live here.

168
00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:50.120
Oh, live here.

169
00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:54.840
A few weeks of summer madness,

170
00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:56.480
or Christmas or...

171
00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.320
But do you know who we were all after?

172
00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:04.800
Every single one of us,
every party guest,

173
00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:06.960
every weekend house guest,

174
00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:09.600
and the locals,

175
00:10:09.720 --> 00:10:11.680
the entire island,

176
00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:17.560
the object of our affection
was always...

177
00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.160
the good doctor sitting right there.

178
00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:27.360
And then he broke ranks

179
00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:28.440
Why?

180
00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:30.560
Who knows?

181
00:10:31.480 --> 00:10:33.280
The adoring natives?

182
00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.640
But, alas, ultimately unattainable.

183
00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.480
He drove us all mad.

184
00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.600
I mean, he is still, of course, enchanting,

185
00:10:43.760 --> 00:10:46.880
but then, oh, then...

186
00:10:46.960 --> 00:10:48.560
(♪ Piano music stops)

187
00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:50.280
...he was irresistible.

188
00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:53.280
Do you remember?

189
00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.720
I have to go.

190
00:10:56.840 --> 00:10:58.080
Oh, no!

191
00:10:58.200 --> 00:10:59.360
It's my... It's my father.

192
00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:00.480
Oh, God.

193
00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:02.240
Well, tell him...

194
00:11:02.400 --> 00:11:05.000
Well, I'm not sure. Tell him
something very unpleasant from me.

195
00:11:05.120 --> 00:11:07.120
And you can have the pleasure
of making it up.

196
00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:08.640
I'm sorry to see you leave.

197
00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.880
- I am so sorry to go.
- Well, stay!

198
00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:12.800
- Yes, stay.
- No, no, no. I really can't.

199
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:16.000
Well, someone could go with you later,
talk the ogre round.

200
00:11:16.120 --> 00:11:17.840
I'm happy to. I love a barney.

201
00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:21.120
No, no, no. I'm not allowed.
But thank you. It's really kind of you.

202
00:11:21.240 --> 00:11:23.920
- Just an hour.
- No, I really, really can't.

203
00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.040
But thank you, everyone, and...

204
00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:29.080
...it was amazing to meet you.

205
00:11:29.200 --> 00:11:31.760
I, um... Sorry, everyone. Sorry. I...

206
00:11:31.880 --> 00:11:32.840
Bye.

207
00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:37.440
Poor girl.

208
00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:41.000
The moment her mother passed away
it all went to hell over there.

209
00:11:41.760 --> 00:11:42.880
Everything's in his name,

210
00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.720
which means it's now all
in the new stepmother's name.

211
00:11:45.800 --> 00:11:47.840
Surely there's a way
to fight that kind of thing?

212
00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:51.240
I mean, she may act young,
but Nina's what?

213
00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:54.920
Twenty-seven, twenty-eight.

214
00:11:55.080 --> 00:11:57.200
I mean, what is she still doing here?

215
00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:01.160
She's either not quite smart enough
to work it all out,

216
00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:02.960
or she's scared of him, or...

217
00:12:04.600 --> 00:12:07.200
Answers on a side of A4, please.

218
00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:09.040
He is nothing but a villain.

219
00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:10.360
Credit where it's due.

220
00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:15.840
You know you can't split the spirit
from a physical being.

221
00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:19.320
I know the play was set in the future

222
00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:23.040
but, still, the universal soul?

223
00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:26.080
I mean that is just,
at the most basic fundamental level,

224
00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.320
actually atomically impossible!

225
00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.280
So maybe Konstantin should change that.

226
00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:31.560
It's poetry.

227
00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:32.840
But it doesn't make any sense.

228
00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:34.440
When he reads it, it does.

229
00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:37.120
I hear you're a writer.

230
00:12:37.240 --> 00:12:39.360
You know what there needs
to be a book about?

231
00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:41.600
The lives of teachers.

232
00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:44.280
Just average teachers.

233
00:12:44.360 --> 00:12:46.000
An average teacher like me.

234
00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:47.920
I mean, trusting you'd do
a bit more research

235
00:12:48.080 --> 00:12:49.640
than some others seem to,

236
00:12:49.800 --> 00:12:52.960
but, you know, I could tell you,
because I'm a teacher myself actually.

237
00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.400
Oh, I'm starting to feel guilty
about Kostya now.

238
00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.800
- I could find him.
- Oh, darling, you're an angel.

239
00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:03.160
Well, I've no idea
why we are all sitting out here still.

240
00:13:03.280 --> 00:13:05.000
We're going to have to accept

241
00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:08.440
that our entertainment for the evening
is sadly over,

242
00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:12.200
and we are forced to entertain ourselves.

243
00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:16.800
You spent half the night on the patio,

244
00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:18.560
not in the house.

245
00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:21.440
I saw you still out there at 3am,
talking to that woman.

246
00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.040
What woman?

247
00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:26.520
- What, Irina?
- And you didn't even notice the cold.

248
00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:28.760
Too distracted, I suppose.

249
00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:30.320
I am 55.

250
00:13:30.440 --> 00:13:32.400
Oh, because that stops a man!

251
00:13:34.200 --> 00:13:38.040
So typical. You're all ready
to fall on your knees for an actress.

252
00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:43.080
Doesn't everyone treat an actress
different from... an accountant?

253
00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:44.520
I mean, artists...

254
00:13:45.440 --> 00:13:47.560
I suppose it's a form of idealism.

255
00:13:48.480 --> 00:13:51.040
Women are always
falling in love with you.

256
00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:53.760
- That idealism too?
- I'm a good doctor.

257
00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:54.840
Oh, yes.

258
00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:57.920
It's that.

259
00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:02.240
You understood it, didn't you?

260
00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.560
A whole octave lower.

261
00:14:11.440 --> 00:14:12.760
Come on, Polina.

262
00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.040
- What are you doing?
- You're a mess.

263
00:14:16.200 --> 00:14:17.640
Must get you a haircut or something.

264
00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:20.560
I know. Always have looked a mess.

265
00:14:20.640 --> 00:14:21.960
Look like a drunk, really.

266
00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:25.960
It's probably why
women never took to me.

267
00:14:26.120 --> 00:14:27.640
And also I feel tired every day,

268
00:14:27.760 --> 00:14:29.480
despite falling asleep
at the drop of a hat.

269
00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:31.680
It's living in this place.
You'd be better off in town.

270
00:14:31.800 --> 00:14:34.160
Oh, God, they'll never give me
any peace here, will they?

271
00:14:34.280 --> 00:14:35.440
Or move back to the mainland.

272
00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.200
They said this place
would be a nice rest.

273
00:14:37.360 --> 00:14:39.080
(Chuckling) A rest?

274
00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:42.400
As soon as I stepped out of the car
it was a bloody onslaught.

275
00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:44.600
They'd save up all their problems
and complaints for me.

276
00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:46.520
I was always ready to leave
by the afternoon.

277
00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:48.200
And now, now...

278
00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:53.240
Now you retire and everyone says
give up the place in the city,

279
00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:56.640
so then, of course,
there's nowhere else for you to go...

280
00:14:56.760 --> 00:14:58.920
Well, for me.
For... For me to go.

281
00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:01.960
- Jealous.
- Oh, come, now.

282
00:15:02.040 --> 00:15:03.480
- Bored and jealous.
- Oh, please.

283
00:15:03.640 --> 00:15:06.320
She hated it before she'd even seen it.

284
00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.040
Hates Nina being on stage,
not her, even...

285
00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:11.520
You imagine this stuff.

286
00:15:11.640 --> 00:15:13.480
Why do you always have
to be so loyal to her?

287
00:15:13.560 --> 00:15:16.200
- Now...
- She has over £70,000.

288
00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:19.320
Just in her normal,
everyday spending account.

289
00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.520
Not savings or assets or investments.

290
00:15:21.600 --> 00:15:24.160
Just 70,000.

291
00:15:25.040 --> 00:15:26.040
Sitting there.

292
00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:27.840
I saw.

293
00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:30.400
Did you know that?

294
00:15:32.640 --> 00:15:34.040
Ask her for a loan, though.

295
00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:37.680
Try it. She'll cry.

296
00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:40.960
Can't you try and enjoy her being here,
enjoy the time?

297
00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:42.280
Your mother worships you.

298
00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:44.000
And I love her!

299
00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:45.920
I'm not trying to upset her,
she just...

300
00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:52.640
Why does she have to live
such a very... pointless life?

301
00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:53.960
Oh...

302
00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:55.280
Museum theatre,

303
00:15:56.160 --> 00:15:57.200
her theatre.

304
00:15:58.280 --> 00:16:00.480
Three walls, some artificial light,

305
00:16:00.600 --> 00:16:02.960
then sit a few hundred people down
to watch people like them

306
00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.040
pretend to be people like them.

307
00:16:06.440 --> 00:16:11.840
Pretend to love and argue
and then find something to say.

308
00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:14.400
A message.

309
00:16:14.520 --> 00:16:17.080
Something short, uncontroversial,
easy to remember.

310
00:16:18.840 --> 00:16:20.320
We need the theatre.

311
00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:22.200
Couldn't...

312
00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:24.600
Couldn't do without it.

313
00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:27.640
Could we?

314
00:16:30.640 --> 00:16:32.040
It needs to be new.

315
00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:35.200
It needs to be more.

316
00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:39.360
It needs to go beyond
what we already know.

317
00:16:40.520 --> 00:16:46.720
It needs some... provocation,
some, some, just some... purpose.

318
00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:49.280
- Else let's get rid of it.
- Now, this writer chap of your mother's.

319
00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:51.560
- What?
- Sorry, weren't you finished?

320
00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:53.720
- No, I'm done.
- Splendid.

321
00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:54.800
What do you make of him?

322
00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:56.880
I can't work him out.
He barely says a word.

323
00:16:57.000 --> 00:16:58.880
- He's...
- He's famous.

324
00:16:58.960 --> 00:17:00.440
- Yes.
- Young.

325
00:17:00.600 --> 00:17:03.920
But after reading Tolstoy
you're not going to need to read Trigorin.

326
00:17:06.040 --> 00:17:07.640
I always wanted to be a writer.

327
00:17:08.440 --> 00:17:09.760
A lifetime ago.

328
00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:13.800
I always wanted to be a writer
and get married.

329
00:17:15.560 --> 00:17:16.760
(Chuckles)

330
00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:19.040
Ah, well.

331
00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:21.680
It would have been nice, though.

332
00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:25.000
Nice to have been
even a second-rate writer.

333
00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:31.880
Everyone left, then?

334
00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:34.040
I'm here.

335
00:17:34.160 --> 00:17:35.640
And, you know,

336
00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:37.440
I may be mad, but, uh...

337
00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:40.080
- l really liked that play.
- Really?

338
00:17:40.200 --> 00:17:44.240
I mean, it was a little strange,
and... I didn't see the end,

339
00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:47.440
and... I didn't understand it,

340
00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:49.520
I mean, all of it,

341
00:17:49.640 --> 00:17:50.880
but I will...

342
00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:53.200
I will remember it.

343
00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:55.480
It's made an impression.

344
00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:57.800
I don't quite know what kind yet, but...

345
00:17:58.760 --> 00:18:00.200
art is meant to do that.

346
00:18:00.320 --> 00:18:03.280
The rest is just fine-tuning.

347
00:18:03.360 --> 00:18:06.000
I'm... I'm glad I saw it.

348
00:18:06.160 --> 00:18:07.720
- Really?
- Yes, really.

349
00:18:07.800 --> 00:18:09.640
- Thank you.
- Yes, I thought it was very good.

350
00:18:09.800 --> 00:18:12.880
- You thought it was good?
- Yes. It was... serious.

351
00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:16.280
Everything tries very hard
not to be serious now.

352
00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:18.280
But nothing can be beautiful
if it's not serious.

353
00:18:18.360 --> 00:18:19.840
Do you think
I should carry on writing?

354
00:18:21.600 --> 00:18:22.560
Yes!

355
00:18:23.520 --> 00:18:24.640
Oh, God, of course, yes.

356
00:18:24.760 --> 00:18:25.760
(Stifled crying)

357
00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:30.320
You are an emotional lot, aren't you?

358
00:18:31.400 --> 00:18:32.680
Look at the state of you.

359
00:18:35.040 --> 00:18:40.000
You know, I've done
a lot of, uh, different things.

360
00:18:40.720 --> 00:18:44.160
I've had a very... varied life, really.

361
00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:47.120
But if I was creative, creative like you,
I'd be a very different man.

362
00:18:47.240 --> 00:18:52.520
I'd have nothing but contempt
for regular life, for my actual life.

363
00:18:53.960 --> 00:18:57.200
Because when you're creating,
you're in another world.

364
00:18:57.320 --> 00:18:58.360
You're...

365
00:18:59.920 --> 00:19:01.960
Well, you're high, aren't you?

366
00:19:02.120 --> 00:19:03.480
- Where's Nina?
- She went home.

367
00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:05.880
You have to make sure
it doesn't lead you by the nose, talent.

368
00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:08.520
- Nina went home?
- You harness your talent, not it you.

369
00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:10.840
- Where's Nina?
- I told you, she went home.

370
00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:12.320
But I need to see her.

371
00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:14.560
- Well, she's gone.
- I've got to talk to her. I can't just...

372
00:19:14.720 --> 00:19:18.320
I'll have to go over there. I'll have to...
Oh, God, it's mad, madness.

373
00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:20.160
I feel...

374
00:19:20.280 --> 00:19:23.840
sick with happiness
just to say her name.

375
00:19:23.920 --> 00:19:25.440
Kostya, please don't go.
Your mother's worried.

376
00:19:25.560 --> 00:19:27.920
I'm gonna see Nina,
and don't follow me, Masha.

377
00:19:28.040 --> 00:19:29.720
- Konstantin.
- She's been chasing me all around...

378
00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:31.720
That's not fair of you.

379
00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.000
Thank you for what you said.

380
00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:37.440
This isn't kind of you.

381
00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:44.040
- Young people.
- Why do you say that?

382
00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:48.400
Because there's nothing else
to say, really?

383
00:19:48.480 --> 00:19:51.000
- Let's go up to the house.
- I have no one to talk to.

384
00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:53.840
I'm so unhappy.

385
00:19:55.880 --> 00:19:58.320
Well, you shouldn't really
talk to me about that.

386
00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:00.240
Who should I to?

387
00:20:00.360 --> 00:20:01.520
My father?

388
00:20:02.520 --> 00:20:03.560
I hate him.

389
00:20:08.440 --> 00:20:09.800
What am I meant to do?

390
00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:13.000
I am making a fool of myself
and I'm going to make it worse.

391
00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:16.760
I feel like I'm going to make it all
so much worse.

392
00:20:17.400 --> 00:20:19.520
I'm making a mess of my life
and I can see it.

393
00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:22.920
- But I don't know what to do.
- About what?

394
00:20:23.040 --> 00:20:24.040
About him.

395
00:20:24.560 --> 00:20:27.160
Well, that's one
neither me nor your father can solve.

396
00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:30.880
I just love him so much.

397
00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:34.560
God, you really are all so dramatic,

398
00:20:35.520 --> 00:20:38.360
so highly strung today.

399
00:20:39.960 --> 00:20:41.960
And such an excess of love.

400
00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:44.800
It must be the moon.

401
00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:49.800
And this... damn lake.

402
00:20:52.520 --> 00:20:54.120
Everyone's feeling so...

403
00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:57.520
romantic.

404
00:21:02.760 --> 00:21:04.120
- Again?
- What?

405
00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:08.240
Again. Black.

406
00:21:08.360 --> 00:21:10.160
I'm in mourning.

407
00:21:10.280 --> 00:21:11.800
- For?
- My life.

408
00:21:12.600 --> 00:21:14.320
- Why?
- I'm unhappy.

409
00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:17.360
Unhappy?

410
00:21:17.480 --> 00:21:19.560
What have you got
to be unhappy about?

411
00:21:20.920 --> 00:21:22.400
Who looks younger?

412
00:21:23.520 --> 00:21:25.240
- Doctor, good doctor?
- Irina.

413
00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.360
I am going somewhere with this,
of course, my darling,

414
00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:29.760
but now answer, in all honesty,

415
00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:31.080
who looks younger?

416
00:21:33.320 --> 00:21:34.960
Why, you do, of course.

417
00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:36.720
And why is that?

418
00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:39.560
Because I'm busy, I do things.

419
00:21:40.120 --> 00:21:43.560
You just stay here,
cut off from the world.

420
00:21:43.680 --> 00:21:47.440
Because this isn't the real world,
or it doesn't need to be your one.

421
00:21:47.520 --> 00:21:48.960
Go, live free,

422
00:21:49.080 --> 00:21:51.400
and stop worrying about the future.

423
00:21:52.200 --> 00:21:53.560
I never look to the future.

424
00:21:53.680 --> 00:21:54.720
What will be will be.

425
00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.880
- ♪ Whatever will be... ♪
- And don't you dare sing, doctor.

426
00:21:58.000 --> 00:22:00.000
I do feel old,

427
00:22:00.120 --> 00:22:02.080
but I don't know
how you change that.

428
00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:06.760
Well, you start
by not letting yourself go.

429
00:22:08.080 --> 00:22:12.520
People round here,
my brother, my son, your parents,

430
00:22:12.600 --> 00:22:15.280
why would I care
what any of you lot think of me?

431
00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:18.280
But have I been slobbing around this place
without my make-up

432
00:22:18.360 --> 00:22:20.760
or in, I don't know, in a...

433
00:22:20.920 --> 00:22:22.080
A hoodie?

434
00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:28.880
Have you ever seen me
not put together, Masha, my love?

435
00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:31.240
No. No one has.

436
00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:36.480
Because these things matter.
You have to take care and have pride.

437
00:22:37.320 --> 00:22:40.400
I feel like
I'm dragging my life behind me.

438
00:22:41.160 --> 00:22:44.040
Like a child
with an old doll or something.

439
00:22:45.320 --> 00:22:48.520
If I got the physicality right,
I could still play Juliet.

440
00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:54.360
Look who's here!
Look who's free!

441
00:22:54.440 --> 00:22:56.960
For three whole days,
we have this girl.

442
00:22:57.120 --> 00:23:01.320
Oh, I have been dying to be here.
I've been so disgustingly jealous.

443
00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:03.800
Plea... please. Please can I just stay
till they get back?

444
00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:06.800
I mean, you probably won't
see me then again for months.

445
00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:09.320
Do try not to pollute my soul
while I'm here,

446
00:23:09.440 --> 00:23:12.080
but, you know, I'm here now.

447
00:23:12.200 --> 00:23:15.080
- That's all that matters.
- We won't let you leave our sight.

448
00:23:15.200 --> 00:23:17.920
It's this lake, I'm drawn back to it.

449
00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:21.440
- In another life I was a seagull.
- (Petrusha chuckles)

450
00:23:23.160 --> 00:23:24.440
Yes.

451
00:23:24.560 --> 00:23:26.240
Take note here.

452
00:23:26.320 --> 00:23:29.560
Locked away in some bizarre
family psychodrama most of her time,

453
00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:31.280
but how does she look?

454
00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:32.760
If Nina can try...

455
00:23:32.920 --> 00:23:35.920
Though I will stop
with the compliments lest I jinx you.

456
00:23:36.000 --> 00:23:38.400
- And where's...
- He's fishing.

457
00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:40.960
- I'm sorry?
- I saw him.

458
00:23:41.120 --> 00:23:44.280
- Fishing? Boris?
- By the lake.

459
00:23:44.360 --> 00:23:45.680
Well, on the lake.

460
00:23:46.560 --> 00:23:48.400
Good God. Isn't he bored yet?

461
00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:53.240
He's either emptied the lake of fish by now
or he's the world's worst fisherman.

462
00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:57.960
And Kostya's down by the lake
the whole time too.

463
00:23:59.160 --> 00:24:03.000
I came all the way down here
and I've hardly seen him.

464
00:24:03.080 --> 00:24:05.400
You can tell me, darling.
What is wrong with him?

465
00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:07.040
He's depressed.

466
00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:08.960
Why don't you do a bit
from his play again, Nina?

467
00:24:09.120 --> 00:24:10.560
Oh, God! Must I?

468
00:24:10.680 --> 00:24:14.960
Ah, that's a shame.
I liked to think you two were united.

469
00:24:15.080 --> 00:24:17.600
Two souls united for a shared goal

470
00:24:17.680 --> 00:24:19.840
in pursuit of an artistic dream.

471
00:24:20.000 --> 00:24:20.800
Oh...

472
00:24:20.920 --> 00:24:23.240
Gave you something
in common though, didn't it?

473
00:24:23.360 --> 00:24:26.680
Because you're both in love.
I mean with each other.

474
00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:29.560
- Unlike us.
- Yes.

475
00:24:29.680 --> 00:24:32.160
But things like that don't matter to me.

476
00:24:32.280 --> 00:24:35.240
I love you despite that,

477
00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:38.600
despite the fact
we've got nothing in common.

478
00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.600
We have no point of...
of contact at all.

479
00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:44.680
I can't stay at home.

480
00:24:44.840 --> 00:24:46.840
I have to walk here every day to you.

481
00:24:46.960 --> 00:24:47.920
I know.

482
00:24:48.000 --> 00:24:49.320
- It's miles.
- I know.

483
00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:51.760
Two miles.

484
00:24:53.000 --> 00:24:56.040
Two miles there and two miles back
and all I get is...

485
00:24:57.480 --> 00:24:58.920
Not that I could blame you.

486
00:24:59.720 --> 00:25:04.280
I have a mother and a brother
and two small sisters

487
00:25:04.400 --> 00:25:07.800
and less than 2,000 a month
before pension contributions.

488
00:25:09.200 --> 00:25:11.280
Who would choose
to fall in love with me?

489
00:25:11.400 --> 00:25:13.160
- And yet here we are.
- Yeah.

490
00:25:13.280 --> 00:25:15.040
- Can we leave this now?
- Leave me loving you?

491
00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:17.080
- You know I'm flattered.
- But you don't return it.

492
00:25:17.200 --> 00:25:19.400
- I can't help that.
- And I can't help this.

493
00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:22.680
But I don't blame you.

494
00:25:22.840 --> 00:25:24.320
Less than 2,000 a month...

495
00:25:24.400 --> 00:25:26.480
- It's not all about money.
- Say those with money.

496
00:25:26.600 --> 00:25:28.480
- You can be poor and happy.
- In theory.

497
00:25:28.640 --> 00:25:30.560
- I'd be a beggar any day rather than...
- Rather than what?

498
00:25:30.640 --> 00:25:32.680
(Petrusha snoring)

499
00:25:35.920 --> 00:25:37.400
- Morning.
- Petrusha!

500
00:25:37.560 --> 00:25:38.920
- Hm?
- Asleep!

501
00:25:39.000 --> 00:25:40.400
Oh, it keeps happening.

502
00:25:41.200 --> 00:25:44.920
I went to bed at nine last night
and got up at ten today

503
00:25:45.040 --> 00:25:47.440
and felt as if my brain
was stuck to my skull.

504
00:25:49.360 --> 00:25:52.080
Asleep after lunch too.
Quite unintentional.

505
00:25:52.200 --> 00:25:54.800
You must go to the doctor, then,
get something for it.

506
00:25:54.920 --> 00:25:56.600
Doctor is there
and he won't give me anything.

507
00:25:56.720 --> 00:25:58.560
I'm not going to start medicating you
at your age.

508
00:25:58.680 --> 00:26:00.480
I'd still like to live, my age or not.

509
00:26:00.600 --> 00:26:01.560
You are alive.

510
00:26:01.640 --> 00:26:04.440
Take a paracetamol when something hurts,
take a nap when you're tired.

511
00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:05.960
Old age is not complicated.

512
00:26:10.080 --> 00:26:11.320
No, it's this place.

513
00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:13.680
The place doesn't suit me.

514
00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:16.840
Never has, actually,
and it seems like it never will.

515
00:26:16.920 --> 00:26:19.320
And I knew that
from the moment I set eyes on it.

516
00:26:19.480 --> 00:26:22.120
Perhaps a spa retreat
would do him some good.

517
00:26:22.200 --> 00:26:23.720
Might.

518
00:26:23.880 --> 00:26:25.120
Might not.

519
00:26:25.240 --> 00:26:26.960
What's that supposed to mean?

520
00:26:27.080 --> 00:26:28.400
Just what I said.

521
00:26:28.520 --> 00:26:30.640
You should give up
smoking and drinking.

522
00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:31.800
(Blows raspberry)

523
00:26:31.960 --> 00:26:34.160
You... You think
you're missing out on life?

524
00:26:34.280 --> 00:26:37.120
Then live a greater portion of it sober.
You're not yourself when you drink.

525
00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:39.160
You start thinking about yourself
in the third person,

526
00:26:39.280 --> 00:26:41.240
you start talking about yourself
in the third person and all.

527
00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:44.280
Oh, it's so easy
for someone like you to say that.

528
00:26:45.040 --> 00:26:48.280
I've seen your house,
like a shrine to your past.

529
00:26:48.400 --> 00:26:49.680
You've lived twenty different lives

530
00:26:49.840 --> 00:26:52.520
and now sit back
and want to talk about philosophy.

531
00:26:52.640 --> 00:26:55.040
I spent my life in a court room.

532
00:26:55.120 --> 00:26:57.440
I barely got a quarter of your life
in more of the time.

533
00:26:57.600 --> 00:27:01.400
A cigar and a sherry, please.
Let me live a tiny life at least. What?

534
00:27:01.520 --> 00:27:03.240
I don't know how
someone gets to your age

535
00:27:03.360 --> 00:27:06.640
and only just realises
that life is a serious business.

536
00:27:06.720 --> 00:27:08.640
You can't start looking
for a cure and sympathy now

537
00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:10.400
for your years of living ungratefully.

538
00:27:10.560 --> 00:27:14.800
How do you ever bear
this incessant boredom, Petrusha!

539
00:27:16.040 --> 00:27:18.640
(Sighing) It's so hot,
and you are all talking God knows what.

540
00:27:18.720 --> 00:27:20.840
What is it? Philosophy?

541
00:27:21.000 --> 00:27:22.360
Just sounds like bickering.

542
00:27:24.000 --> 00:27:25.440
There's no one here.

543
00:27:26.200 --> 00:27:27.480
There's nothing to do.

544
00:27:28.400 --> 00:27:29.680
Nothing to see.

545
00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:34.640
I haven't even got a proper phone signal
unless I practically row out onto the lake.

546
00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:37.040
I mean,
I am glad to be with you all,

547
00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:39.040
but, given the choice,

548
00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:42.640
I'd much rather be
in a hotel room learning lines.

549
00:27:43.200 --> 00:27:44.480
I can imagine.

550
00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:51.280
Of course,
we'd all like to live in the city.

551
00:27:51.360 --> 00:27:52.160
Would we?

552
00:27:52.320 --> 00:27:54.320
No one just turns up like here.

553
00:27:54.440 --> 00:27:56.040
And then you can't get rid of them.

554
00:27:56.560 --> 00:27:58.160
People call first.

555
00:27:58.920 --> 00:28:01.040
And you don't wait an hour
for a taxi to arrive

556
00:28:01.160 --> 00:28:03.120
because they're out there
in the street, and...

557
00:28:04.720 --> 00:28:05.920
all sorts of things.

558
00:28:06.080 --> 00:28:09.280
So, here you all are!

559
00:28:10.000 --> 00:28:12.400
Everyone's looking very relaxed.

560
00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:16.720
- Now, my wife says...
- Yes?

561
00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:20.040
She says you two
are going to the mainland today.

562
00:28:20.160 --> 00:28:21.120
Yes.

563
00:28:21.240 --> 00:28:23.200
And how you doing that, then?

564
00:28:23.360 --> 00:28:25.280
- I'm sorry?
- Well, I'm just asking.

565
00:28:25.360 --> 00:28:27.480
I'm asking you
because I already asked her.

566
00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:29.800
How you doing that, then?

567
00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:32.640
- With the ferry, of course.
- Oh, of course.

568
00:28:33.680 --> 00:28:34.960
And how you getting there?

569
00:28:35.800 --> 00:28:37.880
With that Land Rover thing,
I suppose.

570
00:28:38.520 --> 00:28:39.640
My Land Rover thing?

571
00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:41.640
Well, can we borrow your Land Rover?

572
00:28:42.360 --> 00:28:43.320
No.

573
00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:45.840
Why?

574
00:28:45.960 --> 00:28:48.640
- I have to go to town.
- Oh, you don't have to go anywhere.

575
00:28:48.760 --> 00:28:51.640
I have to go to town.
Place don't run itself.

576
00:28:51.760 --> 00:28:53.040
Can't all be lying round.

577
00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:54.320
Use the Jeep.

578
00:28:54.440 --> 00:28:56.920
The Jeep? The fucking Jeep?

579
00:28:57.040 --> 00:28:58.720
You want me to go to town in the Jeep?

580
00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:01.120
Jeep won't make it up the road.
Use the Jeep?

581
00:29:01.240 --> 00:29:03.360
You use the Jeep.
And God be with you both.

582
00:29:03.480 --> 00:29:06.840
So, I am marooned here
because you want to go into town?

583
00:29:06.920 --> 00:29:09.520
I want to? I don't want to, I have to.

584
00:29:09.680 --> 00:29:12.160
You want to.
You're the one wanting things.

585
00:29:12.280 --> 00:29:15.840
Uh, you two girls wanna go shopping,
you're welcome to come with me.

586
00:29:15.960 --> 00:29:17.360
There are shops in town.

587
00:29:17.480 --> 00:29:19.680
I'd rather die than shop in that town.

588
00:29:20.280 --> 00:29:22.320
Well, it's death in town
or lying round here, then,

589
00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:23.760
cos the mainland's not happening.

590
00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:26.600
Do you know what? It's the same
every time I come here, Petrusha.

591
00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:28.080
I come to see you and my son

592
00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:31.240
and your staff conspire
to trap me here.

593
00:29:31.320 --> 00:29:32.800
It is barbaric.

594
00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:34.120
I am going home.

595
00:29:34.200 --> 00:29:35.600
Ah, yeah, course you are!

596
00:29:35.720 --> 00:29:37.800
You hear that, Polina?
She's leaving now.

597
00:29:37.920 --> 00:29:40.920
I am going home today,
and it's thanks to you, Mr Shamrayev.

598
00:29:41.080 --> 00:29:42.960
I'm just doing
what you pay me to do.

599
00:29:43.080 --> 00:29:45.480
You can pay me
to lie around with you lot if you want to.

600
00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:47.080
If you won't lend me the Land Rover,

601
00:29:47.160 --> 00:29:49.360
- I shall walk to the ferry.
- If that's what you're doing...

602
00:29:49.520 --> 00:29:52.240
I shall swim back to civilisation
if I have to.

603
00:29:52.320 --> 00:29:53.840
Well, go and get your cozzie on, then.

604
00:29:54.840 --> 00:29:56.880
Civilisation's waiting for you.

605
00:29:57.800 --> 00:29:59.200
Yeah.

606
00:29:59.360 --> 00:30:00.720
In fact, better than that,

607
00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.760
take the Land Rover cos I'm done.

608
00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:04.520
I'm done. I resign.

609
00:30:04.640 --> 00:30:05.760
I'm resigning, yeah?

610
00:30:05.880 --> 00:30:08.880
- Yeah?
- Yeah. I am done, and I mean it.

611
00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:10.120
I mean it this time.

612
00:30:10.200 --> 00:30:11.200
I'm done running round

613
00:30:11.360 --> 00:30:17.320
after you ungrateful, deluded,
townie, fucking...

614
00:30:18.640 --> 00:30:21.280
fucking... socialists.

615
00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:25.520
You can all go and...
find yourself another manager.

616
00:30:28.720 --> 00:30:31.200
I take his insults and abuse every year.

617
00:30:32.000 --> 00:30:34.560
Every year, Petrusha,
but I can't take it anymore!

618
00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:38.080
I cannot take a man
shouting and screaming at me!

619
00:30:39.160 --> 00:30:41.640
Shouting and screaming over a car!

620
00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:45.680
A car, of all things! I mean,
where is anyone's perspective?

621
00:30:45.800 --> 00:30:47.640
I'm never coming
to this godforsaken place again.

622
00:30:47.720 --> 00:30:49.680
No, Irina, Irina... Oh.

623
00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:52.320
This is madness.
Have all the cars brought up here.

624
00:30:52.440 --> 00:30:53.480
She can take her pick.

625
00:30:53.600 --> 00:30:56.400
I don't understand.
I mean, don't you work for them?

626
00:30:56.520 --> 00:30:59.600
For him, I mean,
with errands in town and...

627
00:30:59.720 --> 00:31:01.040
What was I meant to do about that?

628
00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:03.840
Come on,
let's go and persuade her to stay.

629
00:31:03.960 --> 00:31:06.080
He doesn't mean it, he never does,

630
00:31:06.160 --> 00:31:08.000
He just does this all the time,

631
00:31:08.160 --> 00:31:09.880
makes a bloody nuisance
of himself.

632
00:31:10.000 --> 00:31:11.960
For the love of God,
why don't they just fire him?

633
00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:13.880
He's getting worse and worse.

634
00:31:14.000 --> 00:31:16.560
- He gets away with it and...
- They'll end up apologising to him.

635
00:31:16.680 --> 00:31:18.000
He's the one that broke the Jeep.

636
00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:19.240
Well, what can I say?

637
00:31:20.080 --> 00:31:23.200
An unpleasant,
bad-tempered, inept man.

638
00:31:24.200 --> 00:31:25.240
And you married him.

639
00:31:27.560 --> 00:31:29.280
There's something like this every day.

640
00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:34.240
Please, can we talk about...

641
00:31:34.360 --> 00:31:35.960
- Now...
- You wouldn't have to do anything.

642
00:31:36.080 --> 00:31:38.120
- I'd just come to you.
- Now...

643
00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:41.040
We're not young anymore.

644
00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:43.240
We keep saying,
"Someday, sometime."

645
00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:45.320
Now.

646
00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:47.800
Let's do it now.

647
00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:49.080
I'm 55.

648
00:31:50.480 --> 00:31:53.160
It feels a bit late for some big upheaval
of everyone's lives.

649
00:31:57.720 --> 00:31:59.160
You can't have them all.

650
00:32:00.400 --> 00:32:01.800
- What's this now?
- All your women.

651
00:32:01.920 --> 00:32:03.160
I know.

652
00:32:03.280 --> 00:32:05.840
You have lots of things like this,
like you do with me.

653
00:32:05.960 --> 00:32:08.040
But you think
you can keep them all open,

654
00:32:08.160 --> 00:32:09.680
all ready for you forever.

655
00:32:10.680 --> 00:32:13.080
Who else is offering
to leave their husband to live with you?

656
00:32:13.160 --> 00:32:14.600
What women are you talking about?

657
00:32:19.320 --> 00:32:20.840
I know it's none of my business.

658
00:32:21.000 --> 00:32:23.440
I'm sorry I spoilt things.

659
00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:27.520
He upset me. I've been stuck with him
and you've been with that woman.

660
00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:29.240
- I have to be with somebody.
- But with her?

661
00:32:29.360 --> 00:32:31.720
- I can't be with you.
- I know. I'm sorry.

662
00:32:32.840 --> 00:32:33.960
Don't say sorry.

663
00:32:35.440 --> 00:32:37.480
I can't expect you
to avoid women entirely, can I?

664
00:32:37.560 --> 00:32:39.160
I mean, you're a doctor,
so how could you?

665
00:32:39.280 --> 00:32:40.320
- Quite.
- But...

666
00:32:41.760 --> 00:32:42.800
But you could...

667
00:32:44.920 --> 00:32:46.000
...try.

668
00:32:46.080 --> 00:32:48.320
- Um...
- Nina!

669
00:32:48.400 --> 00:32:50.040
What's going on up there?

670
00:32:50.200 --> 00:32:52.400
Uh, she's hysterical.

671
00:32:52.520 --> 00:32:54.840
And, um, he has asthma.

672
00:32:54.920 --> 00:32:57.520
Oh. Well, I'd better go up there.

673
00:32:57.680 --> 00:33:01.840
It is so strange
to see someone famous cry.

674
00:33:01.920 --> 00:33:04.240
- I mean for real, not acting.
- Hm.

675
00:33:04.400 --> 00:33:08.000
Over something,
just something ordinary.

676
00:33:08.160 --> 00:33:10.480
And she cries like she does on TV.

677
00:33:10.560 --> 00:33:11.640
Does she?

678
00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:13.560
Are we alone?

679
00:33:14.320 --> 00:33:17.600
- (Nina) Your mother is in such a state.
- (Konstantin) When isn't she?

680
00:33:17.680 --> 00:33:22.960
Well, there was this big argument,
about the cars and it's...

681
00:33:23.120 --> 00:33:26.120
Well, she is in tears!

682
00:33:26.800 --> 00:33:28.120
She's crying.

683
00:33:28.240 --> 00:33:30.360
- And she is so...
- What?

684
00:33:30.440 --> 00:33:31.760
- Famous!
- Yes.

685
00:33:32.560 --> 00:33:34.680
Boris Trigorin is still by the lake.

686
00:33:34.840 --> 00:33:36.200
He's been there all day.

687
00:33:36.320 --> 00:33:38.520
He's delighted to have caught a fish.

688
00:33:38.680 --> 00:33:39.840
Isn't that mad?

689
00:33:39.960 --> 00:33:41.840
- Because he is so...
- Famous.

690
00:33:41.960 --> 00:33:42.920
Yes.

691
00:33:44.760 --> 00:33:50.040
His books have been translated
into over 34 different languages.

692
00:33:50.200 --> 00:33:52.040
I looked that up. Did you know that?

693
00:33:52.160 --> 00:33:55.280
And there was
this whole documentary about him

694
00:33:55.400 --> 00:33:58.680
with other famous people
talking about him,

695
00:33:58.840 --> 00:34:01.720
and... and he is just by the lake,

696
00:34:02.720 --> 00:34:03.760
fishing!

697
00:34:04.920 --> 00:34:06.440
Your mother was in it.

698
00:34:06.600 --> 00:34:07.800
The documentary, I mean.

699
00:34:07.880 --> 00:34:09.240
- You watched it?
- Yeah, yeah.

700
00:34:09.320 --> 00:34:11.240
It was all about him, and...

701
00:34:11.920 --> 00:34:13.760
And he is just here.

702
00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:17.480
And she is just here.

703
00:34:17.600 --> 00:34:19.400
And it's just...

704
00:34:19.560 --> 00:34:21.200
I don't know, I just...

705
00:34:21.320 --> 00:34:24.560
I thought they'd be all...
remote or something.

706
00:34:24.680 --> 00:34:26.320
You know? Like, above all of this.

707
00:34:26.400 --> 00:34:31.200
Fighting over cars and fishing
and losing their tempers

708
00:34:31.320 --> 00:34:32.840
like they were just anybody.

709
00:34:33.520 --> 00:34:36.120
Just... ordinary.

710
00:34:36.880 --> 00:34:38.040
I mean, don't you think?

711
00:34:40.040 --> 00:34:41.600
You know...

712
00:34:41.680 --> 00:34:43.120
I suppose you are used to it.

713
00:34:49.160 --> 00:34:50.320
What's in the bag?

714
00:34:58.480 --> 00:34:59.600
What is it?

715
00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:02.520
I shot it.

716
00:35:04.040 --> 00:35:05.080
Why?

717
00:35:07.480 --> 00:35:09.240
This is what I'd do for you.

718
00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:16.640
Look how low I can go for you.

719
00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:22.760
I present my sin at your feet.

720
00:35:25.840 --> 00:35:27.080
What's wrong with you?

721
00:35:31.280 --> 00:35:34.840
I'll shoot myself soon,
just like that.

722
00:35:37.080 --> 00:35:38.120
Would you like that?

723
00:35:41.640 --> 00:35:44.280
I'm just an embarrassment now,
aren't I?

724
00:35:44.360 --> 00:35:46.640
Now the real thing is here,
I'm just... now I'm just...

725
00:35:46.760 --> 00:35:49.240
It's not me that's changed, it's you.

726
00:35:50.400 --> 00:35:51.760
Ever since they got here

727
00:35:51.840 --> 00:35:55.880
it's all signals and symbols
and saying one thing by doing another.

728
00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:59.240
How am I meant to understand
if you don't make any sense?

729
00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:01.120
Like this.
What is this meant to mean?

730
00:36:01.240 --> 00:36:03.840
Sorry I'm not clever enough
to work it out.

731
00:36:03.960 --> 00:36:05.480
What does the dead bird mean?

732
00:36:05.600 --> 00:36:08.640
Women can forgive
anything but failure.

733
00:36:09.600 --> 00:36:13.360
The play went wrong,
it was a failure, I was a failure.

734
00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:15.640
I've burnt it. That make it better?
I burnt the play.

735
00:36:16.520 --> 00:36:18.360
- Burnt it?
- Yes, yes, I burned it.

736
00:36:19.960 --> 00:36:21.800
I don't know
what you want me to say.

737
00:36:23.840 --> 00:36:25.920
You have no idea,

738
00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:27.920
no clue of just how...

739
00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:31.800
...how...

740
00:36:34.440 --> 00:36:35.720
... how unhappy I am.

741
00:36:38.000 --> 00:36:39.560
- I am sorry.
- Are you?

742
00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:40.720
Because it's you. It's you.

743
00:36:40.880 --> 00:36:44.480
All detached and...
and shocked and distant.

744
00:36:45.720 --> 00:36:47.120
Who is this?

745
00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:49.440
Who is this Nina in front of me?

746
00:36:49.560 --> 00:36:52.320
"What's this meant to mean? Sorry
I'm not clever enough to work it out."

747
00:36:52.440 --> 00:36:54.240
There's nothing to work out!

748
00:36:54.360 --> 00:36:55.680
You hate me.

749
00:36:55.800 --> 00:36:58.000
My play was a disaster.

750
00:36:58.160 --> 00:37:00.120
You think I'm a nobody.

751
00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:01.840
Just like the rest of them.

752
00:37:02.520 --> 00:37:04.920
Like I'm just famous Irina's son.

753
00:37:06.120 --> 00:37:07.120
You are her son.

754
00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:10.280
Oh, here he comes.

755
00:37:10.440 --> 00:37:12.960
Here comes the real deal.

756
00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:17.280
I was the fake, the placeholder,

757
00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:18.840
and now you're done with me.

758
00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:22.960
If you knew
how you were looking at him, Nina.

759
00:37:23.040 --> 00:37:24.680
Please don't embarrass me.

760
00:37:27.760 --> 00:37:29.080
I won't interrupt.

761
00:37:30.720 --> 00:37:31.680
I'll go.

762
00:37:34.440 --> 00:37:35.440
Hello.

763
00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:37.640
- Oh.
- I mean, morning.

764
00:37:37.800 --> 00:37:39.200
Yes, yes, morning.

765
00:37:39.280 --> 00:37:41.960
I hear we're leaving,
me and Irina, apparently.

766
00:37:42.840 --> 00:37:44.480
Because of the argument?

767
00:37:45.320 --> 00:37:47.240
You're really leaving because of that?

768
00:37:47.400 --> 00:37:49.240
I don't... I wasn't there.
Are we not?

769
00:37:49.360 --> 00:37:50.320
No... No, no.

770
00:37:51.000 --> 00:37:52.320
No, no, I mean...

771
00:37:52.440 --> 00:37:53.840
Well, you'll know better than...

772
00:37:54.360 --> 00:37:57.200
You know, if you've heard you're leaving,
then you must be.

773
00:37:57.320 --> 00:37:58.560
I don't suppose we'll meet again.

774
00:37:58.680 --> 00:38:00.320
Or at least not...
If I came again, I...

775
00:38:00.480 --> 00:38:04.160
But I think it's more likely
we don't come across one another than do.

776
00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:06.080
Well, I'll just be here, so...

777
00:38:06.160 --> 00:38:08.560
It's not often I meet
interesting women your age.

778
00:38:08.640 --> 00:38:10.160
Well, our...

779
00:38:10.280 --> 00:38:12.000
I'm sure that's not true.

780
00:38:12.120 --> 00:38:13.920
I never seem to get them quite right.

781
00:38:14.040 --> 00:38:16.320
- Women?
- No. Well, I mean, in my...

782
00:38:16.440 --> 00:38:17.600
- In my...
- Books?

783
00:38:17.680 --> 00:38:19.120
Yes, in my books.

784
00:38:19.680 --> 00:38:21.600
They never sound quite right.

785
00:38:21.720 --> 00:38:23.120
Wish I could be you for just an hour.

786
00:38:23.240 --> 00:38:24.480
I wish I could be you.

787
00:38:24.600 --> 00:38:25.920
Ah, yeah, course.

788
00:38:26.480 --> 00:38:27.760
No, sorry, why's that?

789
00:38:31.040 --> 00:38:33.800
Well, to be famous.

790
00:38:34.840 --> 00:38:36.120
You know, and a writer.

791
00:38:36.280 --> 00:38:39.560
Like, a celebrated writer.

792
00:38:40.640 --> 00:38:42.440
Look, I'm not... I'm not being...

793
00:38:45.520 --> 00:38:47.640
Just to see what it's like.

794
00:38:47.720 --> 00:38:50.320
Like, what does it feel like?

795
00:38:51.440 --> 00:38:52.720
To be famous.

796
00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:56.000
Well, it doesn't really feel like anything.

797
00:38:56.160 --> 00:38:58.000
Either that or I'm not really very famous.

798
00:38:58.760 --> 00:39:01.360
I mean, I'm not chased down the street.
People don't recognise me.

799
00:39:02.000 --> 00:39:04.320
- I recognise you.
- Well, you've... you've met me.

800
00:39:08.920 --> 00:39:13.640
No. No, no, I...
I would recognise you anywhere.

801
00:39:13.760 --> 00:39:16.240
I mean, people must recognise you.

802
00:39:16.400 --> 00:39:18.560
Yes, well, I just mean
I can live quite normally.

803
00:39:18.640 --> 00:39:19.960
Fame's a tiny...

804
00:39:20.080 --> 00:39:22.280
What's it like to read about yourself?

805
00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:25.520
When they're nice, it's nice,
and when they're not, it's not.

806
00:39:27.960 --> 00:39:28.920
Wow.

807
00:39:31.320 --> 00:39:32.280
Not really.

808
00:39:32.360 --> 00:39:34.640
No. No, no, no.

809
00:39:35.800 --> 00:39:37.080
No.

810
00:39:37.240 --> 00:39:38.400
But it's just...

811
00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:42.440
It's just that sometimes you realise

812
00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:46.320
just how different
everyone's lives are from one another.

813
00:39:47.520 --> 00:39:52.520
You know, like, we're two people,
in the same place, at the same...

814
00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:57.520
But... But I live
this boring, ordinary, little life

815
00:39:57.680 --> 00:40:02.720
and... and I'm here
and I'll just be someone you met once.

816
00:40:02.880 --> 00:40:03.960
Cos you're leaving

817
00:40:04.120 --> 00:40:08.960
to this one-in-a-million life.

818
00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:11.840
Like, I'm not... I'm not being...

819
00:40:12.000 --> 00:40:14.200
You know, you're... you're brilliant.

820
00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:17.600
And so you deserve a brilliant life.

821
00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:19.320
I'm not... I'm saying all the wrong things.

822
00:40:19.400 --> 00:40:22.400
No, it's just, it doesn't feel like that.

823
00:40:22.560 --> 00:40:24.760
You know, cos I'm not just talking
about money,

824
00:40:24.880 --> 00:40:27.560
or fame, cos you're not just famous.

825
00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:31.040
You are a writer.

826
00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:34.560
You're a celebrated writer.

827
00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:39.000
You have this thing
that you give to the world

828
00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:41.400
and then everyone loves it.

829
00:40:41.560 --> 00:40:44.080
- Well, you for it.
- I know you don't mean anything by this.

830
00:40:44.160 --> 00:40:47.840
You know, you have
a passion and a purpose

831
00:40:48.000 --> 00:40:50.480
and you... you're good at it.

832
00:40:50.600 --> 00:40:52.880
And so, then, you know,
because of those of three things

833
00:40:53.000 --> 00:40:55.200
you just get
to be rich and famous as well.

834
00:40:55.320 --> 00:40:57.600
I know you're trying to be nice
but I don't think I, um...

835
00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:02.040
I don't think I have the brilliant,
interesting life you wish I had.

836
00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:03.160
You do.

837
00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:05.600
I fail to notice it.

838
00:41:07.560 --> 00:41:09.160
I should...
I should do some work now.

839
00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:10.480
Yes, yes.

840
00:41:10.640 --> 00:41:12.600
- If... If we are leaving soon.
- Of course, sorry.

841
00:41:12.760 --> 00:41:13.840
- Yes, I'll, um...
- I'll...

842
00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:17.280
I... No, you see, you've, um...
You've hit the... the... the...

843
00:41:17.840 --> 00:41:21.400
It's one of my little things.
My... um...

844
00:41:21.520 --> 00:41:24.320
My pet hate... No, not...

845
00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:27.080
- What?
- It's a sore point.

846
00:41:27.200 --> 00:41:28.800
Lucky to be a writer.

847
00:41:28.920 --> 00:41:30.360
- I mean, it's...
- Oh.

848
00:41:30.520 --> 00:41:31.760
It's an obsession.

849
00:41:32.760 --> 00:41:35.440
You know the man who can think
of nothing but the moon? It's like that.

850
00:41:35.600 --> 00:41:37.640
Kind of.
It's kind of like that, only not.

851
00:41:39.840 --> 00:41:42.560
I am standing, sitting, here with you,

852
00:41:42.720 --> 00:41:44.360
and I want to talk to you.

853
00:41:45.200 --> 00:41:48.320
I want to, I want all these things,
but back of my head is going,

854
00:41:48.480 --> 00:41:51.760
"I must write. I must write."
Get that story down before you lose it.

855
00:41:51.920 --> 00:41:53.840
Then another,
then another, then another.

856
00:41:53.960 --> 00:41:56.240
You have the thought for another
in the middle of the other

857
00:41:56.360 --> 00:41:57.800
and I have to get it down
to the page,

858
00:41:57.960 --> 00:42:00.200
and as soon as I've finished one,
here comes another.

859
00:42:00.320 --> 00:42:01.280
It's like...

860
00:42:01.440 --> 00:42:05.400
It's like... It's like vomiting,
again and again.

861
00:42:05.560 --> 00:42:08.080
And I mean, what's so brilliant about...?
Do you know what I just thought?

862
00:42:08.160 --> 00:42:11.240
See, behind you, look,
there's a cloud shaped like grand piano.

863
00:42:11.400 --> 00:42:14.000
My thoughts just got interrupted
by me thinking I must remember that,

864
00:42:14.120 --> 00:42:17.280
must use that, can't just see that, and...
I mean, what's so great about that?

865
00:42:17.400 --> 00:42:19.880
It's not a story, it's not an idea,
it's a thing I saw.

866
00:42:20.360 --> 00:42:22.080
I don't need to log that, use that.

867
00:42:23.040 --> 00:42:25.640
I can smell heliotropes.
You know heliotropes, the flowers?

868
00:42:25.800 --> 00:42:28.440
And I'm going,
"Sweet, sickly smell. Use that.

869
00:42:29.200 --> 00:42:31.480
"Use that. Use that when describing
a summer's evening."

870
00:42:32.440 --> 00:42:33.600
Everything you say...

871
00:42:34.400 --> 00:42:35.840
...to be used.

872
00:42:36.000 --> 00:42:38.480
I can't go to the theatre or read or...

873
00:42:38.600 --> 00:42:40.400
It's as if I'm eating my own life.

874
00:42:42.360 --> 00:42:43.960
Do you know what I mean?
Does that make sense?

875
00:42:45.040 --> 00:42:47.640
And for what? For what, Nina?
It is Nina, right?

876
00:42:47.800 --> 00:42:49.160
Yes, yeah.

877
00:42:51.200 --> 00:42:54.560
What am I cannibalising
even the most mundane things for, Nina?

878
00:42:54.720 --> 00:42:56.640
Is it for my great art?

879
00:42:56.760 --> 00:42:58.960
My great books
that everyone keeps asking me about,

880
00:42:59.120 --> 00:43:00.400
saying, "What's next?"

881
00:43:00.560 --> 00:43:02.320
Well, art is sacrifice, isn't it?

882
00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:03.920
Yes, but I'm not very good.

883
00:43:04.600 --> 00:43:06.080
No, I'm not.

884
00:43:06.160 --> 00:43:07.680
You are!

885
00:43:07.800 --> 00:43:10.280
I just, I feel like
you all have to say that.

886
00:43:11.400 --> 00:43:15.440
Is the whole world humouring me
and I'm as bad a writer as I think I am

887
00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:20.000
or am I wonderful
and I just think I'm terrible?

888
00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:21.720
Because then I'm crazy.

889
00:43:21.880 --> 00:43:23.040
But you...

890
00:43:23.160 --> 00:43:26.720
You, like... I can't say anything to...

891
00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:28.720
I...

892
00:43:28.800 --> 00:43:31.680
I don't know how someone cannot know
they are amazing.

893
00:43:31.800 --> 00:43:33.120
I don't trust this...

894
00:43:34.280 --> 00:43:36.400
this constant
"What are you scribbling away at?

895
00:43:36.480 --> 00:43:38.120
"What's the next great masterpiece?"

896
00:43:38.240 --> 00:43:40.880
Everyone says
everything is gonna be great.

897
00:43:41.000 --> 00:43:42.440
Everything will be a success.

898
00:43:43.280 --> 00:43:46.560
Have I not written one bad word
in the last, what, seven, eight years?

899
00:43:48.280 --> 00:43:51.200
I feel there are doctors
creeping up behind me

900
00:43:51.360 --> 00:43:54.040
and a Mental Health Act form
being signed somewhere for me right now.

901
00:43:56.000 --> 00:43:59.240
I'm a bee making honey
and I'm throwing it down the void.

902
00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:01.720
I'm tearing off the tops
of all the best flowers

903
00:44:01.840 --> 00:44:05.600
and I'm left with nothing
and the rest of the world an average book.

904
00:44:05.680 --> 00:44:08.080
Average?

905
00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:11.240
You couldn't be this famous
if you were average.

906
00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:12.400
That's not true.

907
00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:15.440
No.

908
00:44:16.920 --> 00:44:20.160
You are an amazing writer.

909
00:44:21.240 --> 00:44:25.640
Maybe... maybe success
has spoilt it a little for you.

910
00:44:25.800 --> 00:44:27.240
Oh, no, it was worse before.

911
00:44:30.120 --> 00:44:31.880
Exactly the same but no money.

912
00:44:37.560 --> 00:44:40.720
And then, when you're not,
you know, famous.

913
00:44:40.880 --> 00:44:44.160
you never meet a reader,
so, imagine, I was terrified of them.

914
00:44:44.280 --> 00:44:46.280
In my head, it was...

915
00:44:46.440 --> 00:44:48.360
If I ever saw someone
with one of my books it was,

916
00:44:48.480 --> 00:44:50.040
if they had dark hair they hated it

917
00:44:50.200 --> 00:44:52.440
and if they were blonde,
they were never going to finish it.

918
00:44:53.880 --> 00:44:55.480
Leave it in a taxi or something.

919
00:44:56.360 --> 00:44:57.800
I actually saw that once.

920
00:44:59.320 --> 00:45:01.760
But surely the actual writing,

921
00:45:01.920 --> 00:45:04.920
like, when you are actually
making something,

922
00:45:05.000 --> 00:45:06.840
- that's... I mean, that's...
- Oh, that's...

923
00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:08.480
That's... That can be nice.

924
00:45:09.120 --> 00:45:11.160
I quite enjoy the proofs,
but then they publish it.

925
00:45:13.440 --> 00:45:15.960
And the moment,
the very moment it's out, I hate it.

926
00:45:17.160 --> 00:45:20.240
I should have done it all differently,
or... maybe not at all.

927
00:45:21.240 --> 00:45:23.520
And everybody reads it
and says, "Yes, yes, it's good.

928
00:45:23.640 --> 00:45:24.800
"It's good.

929
00:45:24.960 --> 00:45:27.000
"Not as good as that or that,
but it's good."

930
00:45:27.480 --> 00:45:31.120
Everyone says, "Oh, Trigorin,
he's good, he's talented, he's charming,

931
00:45:31.280 --> 00:45:32.840
"but he's not brilliant."

932
00:45:33.000 --> 00:45:34.520
And I know I can see that.

933
00:45:34.640 --> 00:45:36.480
Now that it's published,
for some reason I can see it.

934
00:45:36.600 --> 00:45:38.880
And I know how to put it right now,
but it's too late.

935
00:45:39.000 --> 00:45:40.440
You could revise them.

936
00:45:40.600 --> 00:45:42.440
After they've been published.

937
00:45:42.560 --> 00:45:46.560
Or... you could write a blog
about the changes.

938
00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:49.440
Cos I would read that. You know, I...

939
00:45:50.400 --> 00:45:52.320
The day I die,

940
00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:56.920
people will write, "Trigorin?
Good but not exactly Tolstoy."

941
00:45:58.680 --> 00:46:00.480
I think you're just spoilt.

942
00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:02.400
- Am I?
- By your own success.

943
00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:04.160
Spoilt by my success?

944
00:46:04.320 --> 00:46:05.400
Yes.

945
00:46:05.560 --> 00:46:07.120
But, Nina, what success?

946
00:46:07.200 --> 00:46:09.200
- Oh, my God!
- No, I wouldn't read me.

947
00:46:10.080 --> 00:46:11.440
I wouldn't like me.

948
00:46:11.520 --> 00:46:13.720
As a person, even less as a writer.

949
00:46:13.840 --> 00:46:15.480
Surely that's not succeeding.

950
00:46:16.320 --> 00:46:18.240
And I know what the problem is.

951
00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:20.840
I don't know
what I'm meant to be writing about.

952
00:46:21.000 --> 00:46:23.920
I mean, I know what I'd like to write about.
I'd like to write about nature.

953
00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:25.920
I could write...

954
00:46:27.080 --> 00:46:29.200
...spend half the book on the weather

955
00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:34.960
before ever getting to a theme
or a story or an inciting incident

956
00:46:35.040 --> 00:46:37.920
or a... a... point.

957
00:46:39.240 --> 00:46:42.040
Because, I know, I'm a citizen,
so I should write about the world,

958
00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:43.400
the troubles of the world.

959
00:46:43.560 --> 00:46:46.800
I should write about politics
or science or society.

960
00:46:46.920 --> 00:46:49.000
I should write something important.
It's my duty as a writer.

961
00:46:49.080 --> 00:46:52.240
So I'm chased
like a... a... a fox by dogs

962
00:46:52.400 --> 00:46:54.440
as I make everyone angry
with everything I don't really mean

963
00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:57.080
because I shouldn't just be writing
about weather and lakes and fish

964
00:46:57.200 --> 00:46:59.120
but I should just be writing
about that because... Does that...?

965
00:47:00.400 --> 00:47:02.400
Because when I write anything else,
I'm just a fraud.

966
00:47:03.720 --> 00:47:04.760
I'm fake.

967
00:47:06.320 --> 00:47:08.760
I think you must be working too hard.

968
00:47:09.800 --> 00:47:11.520
You...

969
00:47:11.640 --> 00:47:15.920
You don't realise
how amazing you are.

970
00:47:19.880 --> 00:47:23.120
You might not feel important,

971
00:47:24.480 --> 00:47:26.120
but you are.

972
00:47:27.560 --> 00:47:29.000
You are.

973
00:47:29.160 --> 00:47:31.840
People love you.

974
00:47:33.920 --> 00:47:38.960
You are a very important writer.

975
00:47:43.400 --> 00:47:44.760
I've read all your books.

976
00:47:46.640 --> 00:47:50.320
You're important... to me.

977
00:47:53.120 --> 00:47:54.560
Oh. I almost believed that.

978
00:47:55.800 --> 00:47:58.680
Please, please believe me.
You know...

979
00:47:58.840 --> 00:48:01.080
Maybe I'm not, qualified,
to tell you about the world,

980
00:48:01.240 --> 00:48:02.720
but to me...

981
00:48:08.080 --> 00:48:13.080
...you are an extraordinary writer.

982
00:48:16.520 --> 00:48:18.640
You know, and to...

983
00:48:18.720 --> 00:48:22.200
to have this feeling of being a writer

984
00:48:22.360 --> 00:48:25.840
or... being an actress...

985
00:48:25.920 --> 00:48:27.760
I'd like to be an actress.

986
00:48:27.920 --> 00:48:32.640
You know, all... all this stuff
that you're talking about,

987
00:48:32.760 --> 00:48:34.880
I would take that.

988
00:48:34.960 --> 00:48:35.920
Worse!

989
00:48:37.160 --> 00:48:39.560
The world could turn against me.

990
00:48:39.720 --> 00:48:41.960
I'd be beaten, I'd be poor.

991
00:48:42.080 --> 00:48:44.920
I'd... live off cat food!

992
00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:49.080
I would take all of your self-doubt.

993
00:48:49.160 --> 00:48:51.280
I would suffer it all,

994
00:48:51.360 --> 00:48:53.600
and I would suffer it happily.

995
00:48:54.640 --> 00:48:56.640
I'd just want...

996
00:48:56.760 --> 00:49:02.960
want what you have in return.

997
00:49:05.880 --> 00:49:07.000
- Fame?
- Fame.

998
00:49:07.120 --> 00:49:10.160
But I'm... I'm not just talking famous.
Fame...

999
00:49:10.280 --> 00:49:11.440
Fame...

1000
00:49:12.800 --> 00:49:14.720
like you have it.

1001
00:49:17.000 --> 00:49:19.800
Real, real...

1002
00:49:23.840 --> 00:49:25.160
...glory.

1003
00:49:29.200 --> 00:49:30.520
Glory?

1004
00:49:30.640 --> 00:49:31.600
Yes.

1005
00:49:32.360 --> 00:49:33.320
I mean it.

1006
00:49:35.920 --> 00:49:36.960
Glory.

1007
00:49:46.280 --> 00:49:47.360
I don't want to leave.

1008
00:49:49.800 --> 00:49:51.400
I mean, it's so beautiful here.

1009
00:49:54.240 --> 00:49:56.360
You see that house
on the other side of the lake?

1010
00:50:01.240 --> 00:50:02.200
Yes.

1011
00:50:04.880 --> 00:50:06.520
I was born there.

1012
00:50:07.800 --> 00:50:09.320
That was my mother's house.

1013
00:50:12.800 --> 00:50:15.080
I know this place from the inside out,

1014
00:50:17.480 --> 00:50:19.680
every inch of this place,

1015
00:50:22.160 --> 00:50:25.160
like an aerial map burned into my eyes.

1016
00:50:29.400 --> 00:50:31.200
So of course you don't want to leave.

1017
00:50:34.400 --> 00:50:35.360
You...

1018
00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:40.800
You live in a magical place.

1019
00:50:44.040 --> 00:50:45.240
What's in the bag?

1020
00:50:48.880 --> 00:50:49.840
The bag?

1021
00:50:52.320 --> 00:50:53.280
The bag.

1022
00:50:56.840 --> 00:50:57.680
It's a seagull.

1023
00:50:57.800 --> 00:50:58.760
(Trigorin sniggers)

1024
00:50:59.720 --> 00:51:00.800
What, dead?

1025
00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:03.480
Konstantin shot it.

1026
00:51:05.680 --> 00:51:06.640
What?

1027
00:51:07.600 --> 00:51:08.760
Idea.

1028
00:51:08.840 --> 00:51:10.280
- Really?
- Hm.

1029
00:51:16.800 --> 00:51:18.040
A young girl,

1030
00:51:22.280 --> 00:51:23.840
a beautiful young girl,

1031
00:51:27.560 --> 00:51:29.000
lives by the lake.

1032
00:51:30.160 --> 00:51:32.160
And she loves the lake like a seagull

1033
00:51:33.680 --> 00:51:36.120
and is as happy and free as one.

1034
00:51:43.760 --> 00:51:46.760
And then, one day,

1035
00:51:47.720 --> 00:51:49.520
a man just comes along

1036
00:51:51.360 --> 00:51:53.120
and, with nothing better to do,

1037
00:51:58.360 --> 00:51:59.360
destroys her.

1038
00:52:01.640 --> 00:52:03.280
Like someone did to that.

1039
00:52:10.840 --> 00:52:13.080
An unusually silent audition this.

1040
00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:14.400
I'm so sorry.

1041
00:52:15.560 --> 00:52:16.920
Come on.

1042
00:52:17.080 --> 00:52:19.800
- Must we leave?
- No, no. We're staying.

1043
00:52:19.880 --> 00:52:20.920
Oh.

1044
00:52:23.840 --> 00:52:24.800
Oh.

1045
00:52:31.120 --> 00:52:32.480
It's a dream.

1046
00:52:46.080 --> 00:52:47.040
(Gunshot)

1047
00:52:48.480 --> 00:52:49.440
It's...

1048
00:52:52.800 --> 00:52:57.240
I'm not telling you this for me, OK?
It's because you're a writer.

1049
00:52:57.960 --> 00:52:59.800
- Yes.
- You can use it.

1050
00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:03.360
I mean, you would have
to change my name, obviously.

1051
00:53:03.480 --> 00:53:06.080
- But you can use it, can't you?
- Um...

1052
00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:08.760
Because men don't have the monopoly
on daytime drinking.

1053
00:53:10.200 --> 00:53:11.720
Women do it too.

1054
00:53:11.880 --> 00:53:13.480
The majority in secret.

1055
00:53:13.560 --> 00:53:15.680
I am the honest minority.

1056
00:53:15.840 --> 00:53:17.280
And I drink whisky.

1057
00:53:17.400 --> 00:53:18.640
Women drink whisky.

1058
00:53:18.720 --> 00:53:19.880
Huh! Shocking!

1059
00:53:22.360 --> 00:53:24.000
But, I mean,

1060
00:53:24.120 --> 00:53:26.240
if he... if he had managed it,

1061
00:53:27.240 --> 00:53:29.080
if he was dead right now,

1062
00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:31.280
I would have done
the same thing myself.

1063
00:53:31.400 --> 00:53:33.520
I would have gone
and got the same gun

1064
00:53:33.680 --> 00:53:34.640
and bang.

1065
00:53:35.360 --> 00:53:37.360
It would all be over

1066
00:53:37.520 --> 00:53:38.960
I'd be free of all of it.

1067
00:53:39.560 --> 00:53:41.400
But... but...

1068
00:53:41.480 --> 00:53:46.080
as it is, I've decided
I'm going to stop all of this.

1069
00:53:46.200 --> 00:53:48.320
I've got to stamp out these feelings.

1070
00:53:48.480 --> 00:53:50.120
And if I would do that, for him,

1071
00:53:50.240 --> 00:53:53.280
if I would be brave enough to do that,
and I would be,

1072
00:53:53.400 --> 00:53:55.960
then I can rip my feelings out at the root.

1073
00:53:56.080 --> 00:53:59.840
And you know...
You know how I'm going to do that.

1074
00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:01.760
Do you? Do you?

1075
00:54:01.840 --> 00:54:03.760
- No.
- You know the teacher?

1076
00:54:04.520 --> 00:54:05.760
The one who follows you about?

1077
00:54:05.880 --> 00:54:07.040
Yes.

1078
00:54:07.120 --> 00:54:09.120
Mr Medvedenko.

1079
00:54:09.240 --> 00:54:10.560
I'm going to marry him.

1080
00:54:11.920 --> 00:54:13.040
- Drastic.
- No!

1081
00:54:14.000 --> 00:54:15.480
No. No, not at all.

1082
00:54:15.640 --> 00:54:18.720
Not at all, because...
because something has to change,

1083
00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:20.280
and nothing else is changing here.

1084
00:54:20.440 --> 00:54:22.960
And once you're married,
who has time for love?

1085
00:54:25.080 --> 00:54:28.720
But, really, there'll be, um...
There'll be so many other things, then.

1086
00:54:28.880 --> 00:54:32.440
And all that...
all that stuff that mattered before,

1087
00:54:32.560 --> 00:54:33.800
I'm sure it doesn't after.

1088
00:54:35.040 --> 00:54:36.800
I'm sorry you're leaving.

1089
00:54:36.960 --> 00:54:38.400
Oh, yes, I, um...

1090
00:54:40.160 --> 00:54:41.360
I don't really want to.

1091
00:54:41.480 --> 00:54:43.600
You should ask her to stay longer, then.

1092
00:54:43.680 --> 00:54:45.120
Oh, no. No, no.

1093
00:54:45.240 --> 00:54:48.960
Not after everything that happened
with, um, like... Kostya.

1094
00:54:49.080 --> 00:54:52.720
And, um, apparently
he wants to fight me.

1095
00:54:53.760 --> 00:54:55.000
Fight me now.

1096
00:54:55.880 --> 00:54:57.320
Have a duel perhaps.

1097
00:54:58.080 --> 00:55:00.320
Seems a bit excessive,
because I didn't think...

1098
00:55:00.400 --> 00:55:02.400
Well, I didn't rea... He didn't...

1099
00:55:02.520 --> 00:55:04.320
I mean, whenever...
whenever I'm around,

1100
00:55:04.440 --> 00:55:05.840
he just talks a lot about new art

1101
00:55:05.960 --> 00:55:07.880
and I don't really know
why it has to be one or the other,

1102
00:55:08.000 --> 00:55:10.360
but... but he thinks so and, um...

1103
00:55:11.920 --> 00:55:12.960
And there you go.

1104
00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:14.800
He's just jealous too.

1105
00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:18.720
Not that... not that Kostya's got
anything to do with me, he's just this...

1106
00:55:18.840 --> 00:55:20.760
He's just... No, no, no, no.

1107
00:55:20.920 --> 00:55:24.400
No, my schoolteacher future husband,

1108
00:55:24.480 --> 00:55:26.040
that is who I should be talking about.

1109
00:55:27.080 --> 00:55:30.160
Well, send me your new book,

1110
00:55:30.320 --> 00:55:31.600
and write in it.

1111
00:55:31.680 --> 00:55:34.080
Not that stuff
you say to anyone, though.

1112
00:55:34.200 --> 00:55:35.160
Stuff to me.

1113
00:55:36.080 --> 00:55:37.720
For Masha,

1114
00:55:37.840 --> 00:55:42.360
who wanders the earth
knowing no one and knowing nothing.

1115
00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:44.760
Yes?

1116
00:55:50.600 --> 00:55:52.800
Say a number, um, any number.

1117
00:55:54.080 --> 00:55:55.040
Five.

1118
00:55:55.400 --> 00:55:59.240
Oh. I've forgotten
if odd numbers meant yes or no.

1119
00:55:59.800 --> 00:56:01.280
To what?

1120
00:56:01.360 --> 00:56:04.640
I'm deciding whether to run away
and be an actress.

1121
00:56:05.600 --> 00:56:06.720
Like that?

1122
00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:08.280
It's the best way I could think of.

1123
00:56:11.240 --> 00:56:12.280
You're leaving.

1124
00:56:20.000 --> 00:56:21.440
Page 121, line 10.

1125
00:56:23.400 --> 00:56:24.840
Page 121, line 10?

1126
00:56:25.840 --> 00:56:29.200
Page 121, line 10.

1127
00:56:30.600 --> 00:56:32.360
- Which book?
- My favourite.

1128
00:56:34.160 --> 00:56:35.520
Which is your favourite?

1129
00:56:36.480 --> 00:56:39.200
Please don't make me guess.
It's cruel to make me guess.

1130
00:56:39.360 --> 00:56:41.120
The first one?
That's the one people always say.

1131
00:56:41.280 --> 00:56:44.160
No, your last book.
That was my favourite.

1132
00:56:45.240 --> 00:56:46.560
Really?

1133
00:56:46.680 --> 00:56:47.800
Yeah.

1134
00:56:51.560 --> 00:56:52.720
You'll remember me?

1135
00:56:55.600 --> 00:56:56.920
Yes, yes, of course.

1136
00:57:00.960 --> 00:57:02.040
You'll think of me?

1137
00:57:04.440 --> 00:57:05.480
I'll think of you.

1138
00:57:07.880 --> 00:57:11.240
I'll think of you from last week
when there was that seagull in the bag.

1139
00:57:11.360 --> 00:57:14.200
Oh, yeah. Seagull in a bag.

1140
00:57:15.200 --> 00:57:16.600
Seagull in a bag.

1141
00:57:19.880 --> 00:57:22.680
Please, please can we talk again
before you leave?

1142
00:57:22.840 --> 00:57:23.840
Please?

1143
00:57:24.400 --> 00:57:26.480
- It's because they all...
- Oh, was that Nina?

1144
00:57:26.600 --> 00:57:28.160
...think they're cleverer than you...

1145
00:57:28.240 --> 00:57:29.400
Sorry, we interrupted.

1146
00:57:29.480 --> 00:57:31.680
...and that you're never going to check,
do the research.

1147
00:57:31.800 --> 00:57:33.120
Do you have any of my books
in the house?

1148
00:57:33.240 --> 00:57:34.200
- What?
- Well, last week...

1149
00:57:34.360 --> 00:57:35.320
My books anywhere?

1150
00:57:35.440 --> 00:57:37.640
...I find a contract for £50 a month.

1151
00:57:37.760 --> 00:57:39.160
Petrusha has some, don't you?

1152
00:57:39.280 --> 00:57:40.160
- What?
- In the study.

1153
00:57:40.320 --> 00:57:41.840
- What was that?
- What was what?

1154
00:57:41.960 --> 00:57:44.840
Oh, OK, so I was saying,
50 quid a month,

1155
00:57:45.000 --> 00:57:46.400
but my sister,

1156
00:57:46.480 --> 00:57:50.800
my sister says her contract
is only 30 with unlimited minutes.

1157
00:57:50.880 --> 00:57:52.320
- Darling.
- If you can believe it.

1158
00:57:52.440 --> 00:57:53.760
Darling, would you give us a minute?

1159
00:57:53.880 --> 00:57:56.000
I need to have a little talk
with my brother here.

1160
00:57:56.160 --> 00:57:57.720
Oh, yeah.

1161
00:57:57.880 --> 00:57:58.840
All right.

1162
00:57:59.800 --> 00:58:01.160
I can take a hint.

1163
00:58:03.080 --> 00:58:04.720
Don't come all the way over with us.

1164
00:58:05.560 --> 00:58:08.240
- You aren't well enough.
- It's a couple of hours.

1165
00:58:08.360 --> 00:58:10.480
I'm so bored here as it is
and now you're going.

1166
00:58:10.560 --> 00:58:13.280
Do I have a choice?
I have to get back.

1167
00:58:15.080 --> 00:58:17.760
What are you even planning
to do on the mainland?

1168
00:58:18.720 --> 00:58:21.400
Wander around and wait for the ferry
to take you back here again?

1169
00:58:21.520 --> 00:58:23.320
Nothing in particular.

1170
00:58:23.440 --> 00:58:25.560
I would just like
not to be stuck on an island,

1171
00:58:25.720 --> 00:58:26.800
not stuck on this estate.

1172
00:58:26.960 --> 00:58:29.680
I need... I need waking up.

1173
00:58:29.760 --> 00:58:31.520
I feel half asleep all the time here.

1174
00:58:32.160 --> 00:58:34.560
As if I were an old cigarette butt
or something.

1175
00:58:34.680 --> 00:58:37.440
(Laughing) A cigarette butt?
Petrusha, really!

1176
00:58:38.880 --> 00:58:41.280
Look, I want to get going and...

1177
00:58:41.400 --> 00:58:42.920
I want for you to stay here.

1178
00:58:44.520 --> 00:58:46.400
Stay here and... and talk to my son.

1179
00:58:46.480 --> 00:58:48.960
- Oh, now...
- Really, honestly, I do, I beg you.

1180
00:58:49.080 --> 00:58:52.720
He needs someone
to take care of him right now.

1181
00:58:53.840 --> 00:58:55.800
Look, I can't pretend
I have all the answers

1182
00:58:55.960 --> 00:59:00.000
as to why he got it into his head
he needed to... do that to himself,

1183
00:59:00.120 --> 00:59:01.760
but it's... it's mainly jealousy.

1184
00:59:01.840 --> 00:59:05.400
- So the sooner I get Boris away...
- Well, I mean, it wasn't... it wasn't just...

1185
00:59:05.480 --> 00:59:06.440
- No?
- No.

1186
00:59:07.480 --> 00:59:09.720
Oh, well, he's never done it before.

1187
00:59:09.880 --> 00:59:12.800
What's changed?
Boris has been the change.

1188
00:59:12.960 --> 00:59:17.120
I never dreamt he'd feel so...
so threatened by him.

1189
00:59:17.200 --> 00:59:19.200
It wasn't just that. It's not just that.

1190
00:59:19.880 --> 00:59:21.720
He's a clever young man
and he's stuck here with me,

1191
00:59:21.800 --> 00:59:24.200
back of beyond, no friends, no money.

1192
00:59:24.360 --> 00:59:27.280
And I couldn't do without him,
really couldn't,

1193
00:59:27.360 --> 00:59:29.960
but also,
there's nothing for him to do here.

1194
00:59:31.320 --> 00:59:33.400
I mean, I have... and he's...

1195
00:59:33.520 --> 00:59:34.480
Well...

1196
00:59:35.440 --> 00:59:37.360
He feels a bit of a spare part.

1197
00:59:38.400 --> 00:59:40.720
He doesn't want
to look like a freeloader.

1198
00:59:42.600 --> 00:59:44.920
I mean, it's scary
quite how easy it is to do nothing.

1199
00:59:45.040 --> 00:59:47.120
It's terrifying, in fact.

1200
00:59:47.240 --> 00:59:48.800
See, he wants...

1201
00:59:48.960 --> 00:59:50.520
He wants to be impressive.

1202
00:59:50.680 --> 00:59:52.440
He has pride.

1203
00:59:52.600 --> 00:59:55.800
Well, the things he puts me through
for his pride!

1204
00:59:55.960 --> 00:59:58.680
And no one's stopping him
from doing something.

1205
00:59:58.760 --> 01:00:01.720
He could do something. He could...
He could join the army.

1206
01:00:01.840 --> 01:00:03.440
- You could give him some money.
- Oh!

1207
01:00:03.560 --> 01:00:06.240
No, I mean... I mean...
I mean, just to sort himself out.

1208
01:00:06.360 --> 01:00:08.240
And maybe dressing like a grown-up,

1209
01:00:08.360 --> 01:00:10.000
like a proper human being might help.

1210
01:00:10.760 --> 01:00:12.800
And... and a holiday, a trip abroad!

1211
01:00:12.880 --> 01:00:16.800
You know the doctor's planning
this great round-the-world extravaganza?

1212
01:00:16.920 --> 01:00:18.920
He should do something like that,
see the world...

1213
01:00:19.080 --> 01:00:20.640
You're just being silly now.

1214
01:00:21.600 --> 01:00:26.040
I live hand to mouth
to be honest, most the time.

1215
01:00:26.120 --> 01:00:27.560
Everything's tied up in other things,

1216
01:00:27.720 --> 01:00:30.320
and to be honest I don't think
I could even do a proper suit right now.

1217
01:00:30.440 --> 01:00:32.000
What? I have no money.

1218
01:00:32.080 --> 01:00:34.880
I'd give it to him if I had it,
but it all goes on this place.

1219
01:00:35.600 --> 01:00:37.520
And then we tried having cows
and they died.

1220
01:00:38.960 --> 01:00:40.320
And then bees and they died.

1221
01:00:41.360 --> 01:00:43.880
There's always some roof, I'm told,
that needs repairing or some...

1222
01:00:44.040 --> 01:00:46.400
And I have to beg that awful man
to let me use my own car!

1223
01:00:46.480 --> 01:00:47.920
Your mansion's falling down!

1224
01:00:48.080 --> 01:00:50.720
Well, you're not the only one
with expenses, Petrusha!

1225
01:00:50.880 --> 01:00:52.720
I know you all like to make out
that it's vanity,

1226
01:00:52.880 --> 01:00:55.000
but the clothes, the networking, the...

1227
01:00:55.120 --> 01:00:57.240
It's all part of the bloody job.
I wish it wasn't!

1228
01:00:57.400 --> 01:00:59.840
Constantly having to play the game,
keep up with it all,

1229
01:00:59.920 --> 01:01:02.400
haemorrhaging money
and living on nothing!

1230
01:01:02.520 --> 01:01:04.880
You know, I'm not sleeping
with bundles of cash

1231
01:01:05.000 --> 01:01:07.000
stuffed under the mattress,
I'm not a bloody gangster.

1232
01:01:07.120 --> 01:01:09.440
- Yes, yes, yes...
- Oh, what's the matter?

1233
01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:12.560
Um... I just feel very...

1234
01:01:12.680 --> 01:01:14.040
The room's just a little bit...

1235
01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:15.480
- Petrusha?
- No, ignore me.

1236
01:01:15.640 --> 01:01:18.840
- Petrusha?
- I mean, the unlimited-minutes deal...

1237
01:01:19.000 --> 01:01:20.840
He says he's not well.
Where's Dorn?

1238
01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:22.600
He said he'd come by!

1239
01:01:22.680 --> 01:01:24.400
Do ambulances even exist here?

1240
01:01:24.560 --> 01:01:26.320
No, don't worry.
He has it sometimes.

1241
01:01:29.960 --> 01:01:32.080
- Are you OK?
- Of course I am. Ignore me, ignore me.

1242
01:01:33.720 --> 01:01:35.320
- You should lie down.
- No, no, it's gone now.

1243
01:01:35.440 --> 01:01:37.160
- All the same.
- All right, all right.

1244
01:01:38.400 --> 01:01:40.040
But I'm still coming to the mainland.

1245
01:01:40.160 --> 01:01:42.840
- We'll see.
- No, we'll not see. I'm coming.

1246
01:01:42.920 --> 01:01:45.680
I'll have a sit-down now
and then I'm coming.

1247
01:01:45.800 --> 01:01:47.920
- Someone go with him.
- No, I'm fine, fine, fine.

1248
01:01:53.280 --> 01:01:54.360
(Petrusha chuckles)

1249
01:01:58.400 --> 01:01:59.360
Shall we?

1250
01:02:00.600 --> 01:02:01.560
Sorry?

1251
01:02:04.720 --> 01:02:06.840
Actually, could you...?

1252
01:02:06.920 --> 01:02:08.080
Could I...?

1253
01:02:10.280 --> 01:02:11.240
Could you...?

1254
01:02:13.760 --> 01:02:14.720
Oh.

1255
01:02:16.440 --> 01:02:19.040
Oh, this reminds me. A riddle.

1256
01:02:19.200 --> 01:02:24.600
What has four legs in the morning,
two at midday and three by the evening?

1257
01:02:27.960 --> 01:02:32.360
Yes, and flat on their back at night.

1258
01:02:32.520 --> 01:02:34.040
We all know that one.

1259
01:02:34.920 --> 01:02:36.040
Oh, God.

1260
01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:37.280
Scared me.

1261
01:02:39.920 --> 01:02:41.560
It's psychosomatic.

1262
01:02:42.520 --> 01:02:45.120
It's this place that does it.

1263
01:02:45.240 --> 01:02:46.280
I know.

1264
01:02:48.800 --> 01:02:50.680
If only someone could have
a fit of generosity

1265
01:02:50.800 --> 01:02:52.440
he could live in the city again.

1266
01:02:52.600 --> 01:02:54.080
(Arkadina sighs)

1267
01:02:54.240 --> 01:02:57.680
He doesn't need much.
A little studio, a bedsit.

1268
01:02:57.800 --> 01:02:59.840
You could say just for a year.
It could be a loan.

1269
01:03:00.000 --> 01:03:02.720
More money. I mean,
where has everyone got this from?

1270
01:03:03.760 --> 01:03:07.680
That I've been hoarding my loot
and now it's payday for you all?

1271
01:03:08.760 --> 01:03:10.880
I'm his sister. I'm your mother.

1272
01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:12.480
And I'm an actress.

1273
01:03:13.560 --> 01:03:16.240
I'm not a banker.
I'm not a financial advisor.

1274
01:03:16.320 --> 01:03:17.840
I'm not a broker.

1275
01:03:17.960 --> 01:03:18.920
I'm not.

1276
01:03:21.240 --> 01:03:22.200
I'm not.

1277
01:03:28.720 --> 01:03:29.880
Now, you won't...

1278
01:03:32.360 --> 01:03:34.280
When we've gone, you won't...

1279
01:03:35.640 --> 01:03:36.600
What?

1280
01:03:39.240 --> 01:03:41.120
You won't go bang-bang again,
sweetheart?

1281
01:03:45.240 --> 01:03:46.400
- No.
- Good.

1282
01:03:48.880 --> 01:03:50.720
It was just a moment of madness.

1283
01:03:51.960 --> 01:03:53.200
I couldn't help it.

1284
01:03:55.080 --> 01:03:56.800
- I won't do it again.
- Good.

1285
01:03:57.920 --> 01:04:00.120
An actual miracle Dorn was here.

1286
01:04:00.200 --> 01:04:01.440
- Medic on the scene.
- I know.

1287
01:04:01.560 --> 01:04:03.640
Imagine the drama if he hadn't been?

1288
01:04:03.760 --> 01:04:06.080
You can't keep anything quiet
round here either.

1289
01:04:06.160 --> 01:04:07.520
Would have been absolute mayhem.

1290
01:04:07.600 --> 01:04:09.680
You've always been good
at that kind of thing.

1291
01:04:09.800 --> 01:04:10.960
What kind of thing?

1292
01:04:11.640 --> 01:04:12.800
Mayhem?

1293
01:04:14.880 --> 01:04:16.120
Drama?

1294
01:04:17.480 --> 01:04:18.720
A crisis.

1295
01:04:21.680 --> 01:04:23.640
It doesn't hurt anymore, does it?

1296
01:04:26.800 --> 01:04:28.920
It can't be very bad now.

1297
01:04:40.440 --> 01:04:42.520
You know, recently,

1298
01:04:42.640 --> 01:04:44.160
the last few days,

1299
01:04:45.800 --> 01:04:47.520
it's been like I was a kid again.

1300
01:04:49.080 --> 01:04:50.320
Don't you think?

1301
01:04:52.200 --> 01:04:55.320
I love you... just as much.

1302
01:04:57.480 --> 01:04:59.000
If it wasn't for that man...

1303
01:05:00.880 --> 01:05:02.720
Why have you let him
get in between us?

1304
01:05:03.760 --> 01:05:07.480
- Because you put him there, darling.
- Because you brought him here, darling.

1305
01:05:07.600 --> 01:05:10.640
- You don't understand him.
- What's there to understand?

1306
01:05:10.760 --> 01:05:11.640
- He's noble.
- Noble?

1307
01:05:11.800 --> 01:05:13.840
- Well, I mean, that's silly.
- No, no fine. He's noble.

1308
01:05:14.000 --> 01:05:17.680
- So noble he's running away.
- Oh, really Kostya. From what?

1309
01:05:17.840 --> 01:05:19.040
From fighting you?

1310
01:05:20.920 --> 01:05:24.120
He's leaving
because I said we were.

1311
01:05:24.280 --> 01:05:26.000
You were never gonna like
any partner of mine,

1312
01:05:26.120 --> 01:05:28.720
but you must respect my freedom
to be with whoever I please.

1313
01:05:28.800 --> 01:05:31.760
You must respect my freedom
to treat them however I please.

1314
01:05:31.880 --> 01:05:34.200
- Oh, this is silly, Kostya!
- Yes, it is!

1315
01:05:35.360 --> 01:05:38.960
We're in here arguing about him
while he... he doesn't care.

1316
01:05:39.840 --> 01:05:41.400
You know where he is right now?
I saw him.

1317
01:05:41.560 --> 01:05:44.840
He's upstairs in my uncle's study
in my uncle's chair, made himself at home.

1318
01:05:44.960 --> 01:05:46.840
He was told to do so in this house.

1319
01:05:46.960 --> 01:05:51.280
Feet up on the desk
and he's reading his own books!

1320
01:05:53.960 --> 01:05:56.760
Probably has Nina at his feet now,

1321
01:05:56.920 --> 01:05:59.160
trying to convince her he's a genius.

1322
01:06:00.400 --> 01:06:02.000
Must have his acolytes.

1323
01:06:03.760 --> 01:06:06.080
Soft target, though,
don't you think, Mother?

1324
01:06:06.160 --> 01:06:07.800
She's never even been off the island.

1325
01:06:08.440 --> 01:06:10.760
Maybe he is a genius after all.

1326
01:06:12.600 --> 01:06:14.000
But yes, you're right, though.

1327
01:06:15.200 --> 01:06:17.960
A really noble nature.
Oh, I can see that now.

1328
01:06:18.840 --> 01:06:20.360
The perfect word.

1329
01:06:20.480 --> 01:06:23.320
You captured him. Noble.

1330
01:06:23.440 --> 01:06:25.560
We are leaving.
What is the need for this?

1331
01:06:26.880 --> 01:06:28.440
You just enjoy upsetting me, don't you?

1332
01:06:28.520 --> 01:06:30.640
What do you...
What do you get out of it?

1333
01:06:31.520 --> 01:06:33.600
Why else subjugate me to this every day,

1334
01:06:33.720 --> 01:06:36.600
all the time, constant attacks
on a man I respect...

1335
01:06:36.760 --> 01:06:40.120
How? Why?
How in hell can you respect him?

1336
01:06:41.960 --> 01:06:44.640
The sad thing is,
I always thought you were clever.

1337
01:06:44.760 --> 01:06:46.640
I thought you could
see through men like that.

1338
01:06:47.240 --> 01:06:48.800
But no, just another of his women,

1339
01:06:48.960 --> 01:06:50.320
just another acolyte.

1340
01:06:51.000 --> 01:06:53.360
Does he know your names
or do you go by numbers?

1341
01:06:55.040 --> 01:06:56.280
He's conned you all.

1342
01:06:56.440 --> 01:07:00.400
Because, Mother,
prepare yourself now, his books...

1343
01:07:00.480 --> 01:07:03.000
His books are very, very, very average.

1344
01:07:03.920 --> 01:07:05.400
They make me sick.

1345
01:07:05.560 --> 01:07:08.640
His aggressive mediocrity
makes me sick.

1346
01:07:08.760 --> 01:07:11.840
As soon as I see one of his books
I want to be violently sick.

1347
01:07:11.920 --> 01:07:14.520
- Oh, this jealousy, Kostya!
- Jealous?

1348
01:07:14.680 --> 01:07:17.480
You are positively green with it.

1349
01:07:18.160 --> 01:07:20.240
That's probably why you're being sick.

1350
01:07:21.040 --> 01:07:24.600
You and your ambitions and pretensions
only hide envy.

1351
01:07:24.760 --> 01:07:26.880
- Petty, petty envy.
- Thank you.

1352
01:07:27.000 --> 01:07:29.480
What else can you do
but sneer at real, actual talent

1353
01:07:29.640 --> 01:07:31.280
when you have none yourself?

1354
01:07:33.120 --> 01:07:34.280
Real talent?

1355
01:07:34.920 --> 01:07:37.200
That man,
if that's real, actual talent,

1356
01:07:37.320 --> 01:07:40.680
if you two represent
real, actual talent together,

1357
01:07:40.800 --> 01:07:42.640
then we're all doomed.

1358
01:07:42.720 --> 01:07:45.040
Pack up culture and humanity right now.

1359
01:07:47.240 --> 01:07:48.200
I...

1360
01:07:50.200 --> 01:07:51.960
I have more talent
than the both of you,

1361
01:07:52.040 --> 01:07:54.360
than all of you,
all of you doing the same thing,

1362
01:07:54.520 --> 01:07:56.720
congratulating
and congratulating each other,

1363
01:07:56.840 --> 01:07:58.480
not letting anything new in,
anything that could actually...

1364
01:07:58.560 --> 01:08:01.040
Letting in what? Letting in you?
You are deluded!

1365
01:08:01.840 --> 01:08:05.280
- Completely and utterly deluded!
- Why are you still here?

1366
01:08:05.400 --> 01:08:07.400
Your pointless, bland little plays
and empty theatres call.

1367
01:08:07.560 --> 01:08:09.040
I have never played in empty theatres!

1368
01:08:09.120 --> 01:08:11.920
You think you are some creative force,
but you're a puppet for hire,

1369
01:08:12.000 --> 01:08:14.960
speaking other people's thoughts,
leeching off other people's talent, their writing.

1370
01:08:15.080 --> 01:08:18.080
Oh, Kostya, please.
You couldn't even write a pantomime!

1371
01:08:19.200 --> 01:08:24.400
You are a deluded, pretentious,
covetous, little scrounger.

1372
01:08:24.560 --> 01:08:26.800
You turn your nose up
at money, at fame,

1373
01:08:26.880 --> 01:08:29.360
because you are obsessed with it,
because you'll never get it.

1374
01:08:29.480 --> 01:08:31.800
You're not possessed
with the ability to ever earn either.

1375
01:08:31.880 --> 01:08:34.000
You're just a nobody!

1376
01:08:34.080 --> 01:08:36.400
Oh, and now he's crying.
Oh, well, go on!

1377
01:08:37.080 --> 01:08:39.440
I am ever so sorry
for telling you the truth.

1378
01:08:39.560 --> 01:08:41.200
It really is pathetic.

1379
01:08:41.360 --> 01:08:44.720
If his books make you sick,
you definitely make me sick.

1380
01:08:44.840 --> 01:08:46.840
(Stifled crying)

1381
01:08:46.960 --> 01:08:48.400
Oh, sorry.

1382
01:08:48.480 --> 01:08:49.440
I'm sorry.

1383
01:08:53.480 --> 01:08:54.720
Oh, come on, now.

1384
01:08:56.160 --> 01:08:57.400
I'm ever so sorry.

1385
01:08:58.880 --> 01:09:00.880
Kostya, I really didn't mean it,
sweetheart,

1386
01:09:01.040 --> 01:09:02.400
I'm ever so sorry.

1387
01:09:06.840 --> 01:09:08.960
You have a horrible, mean mother,
don't you?

1388
01:09:15.240 --> 01:09:16.480
You don't understand.

1389
01:09:19.200 --> 01:09:21.600
- What don't I understand?
- You just don't understand.

1390
01:09:22.640 --> 01:09:24.200
Well, explain it to me.

1391
01:09:33.240 --> 01:09:34.640
I can't write anymore.

1392
01:09:34.760 --> 01:09:36.120
- Well...
- She doesn't love me.

1393
01:09:36.280 --> 01:09:38.240
- Oh, no, no. Don't say that.
- She doesn't.

1394
01:09:38.320 --> 01:09:39.560
- She doesn't.
- Well, I...

1395
01:09:40.400 --> 01:09:41.680
I'm... I'm...

1396
01:09:44.360 --> 01:09:46.080
I'm taking him away, aren't I?

1397
01:09:48.800 --> 01:09:51.480
She won't see him anymore.
It'll all be OK. We're going.

1398
01:09:54.640 --> 01:09:57.320
And then there's no need
for you to fight him.

1399
01:09:57.440 --> 01:09:58.400
Yes?

1400
01:09:58.480 --> 01:09:59.800
We'll leave.

1401
01:10:01.760 --> 01:10:02.800
Yes.

1402
01:10:06.440 --> 01:10:08.080
And we've made up now?

1403
01:10:11.240 --> 01:10:12.480
- Yes.
- Yes?

1404
01:10:14.520 --> 01:10:16.880
Yes. I don't want to see him, though.
- No, I know.

1405
01:10:18.440 --> 01:10:19.520
Else I will.

1406
01:10:20.960 --> 01:10:22.160
OK. OK.

1407
01:10:24.320 --> 01:10:26.800
"If you ever have need of my life,
come and claim it."

1408
01:10:26.880 --> 01:10:28.040
Oh.

1409
01:10:29.560 --> 01:10:31.680
Page 121, line 10.

1410
01:10:31.800 --> 01:10:32.960
What?

1411
01:10:33.040 --> 01:10:35.440
I wrote that, apparently.

1412
01:10:36.680 --> 01:10:39.160
Page 121, line 10,
in the book you didn't like.

1413
01:10:39.280 --> 01:10:41.680
I did. Don't start this. I did like it.

1414
01:10:41.840 --> 01:10:43.400
Just not as much
as some of my other ones.

1415
01:10:43.520 --> 01:10:45.440
Well, perhaps I am just less fickle
than the average reader.

1416
01:10:46.280 --> 01:10:49.080
The ferry leaves soon.
Let's... Let's get out of here.

1417
01:10:49.240 --> 01:10:50.960
- Let's go and...
- Let's...

1418
01:10:51.080 --> 01:10:52.240
What?

1419
01:10:53.360 --> 01:10:56.360
Let's stay another... another day.

1420
01:10:57.600 --> 01:10:58.640
No.

1421
01:10:59.520 --> 01:11:00.480
No?

1422
01:11:01.560 --> 01:11:02.520
No.

1423
01:11:03.360 --> 01:11:04.320
Just...

1424
01:11:05.960 --> 01:11:06.920
No?

1425
01:11:09.040 --> 01:11:11.040
I know why you want to stay, and...

1426
01:11:14.000 --> 01:11:15.840
Please, you must get a hold of yourself.

1427
01:11:15.960 --> 01:11:18.240
You need to... sober up a little.

1428
01:11:18.360 --> 01:11:19.760
- Sober?
- Sober, yes. Sober. Be sober.

1429
01:11:19.880 --> 01:11:21.600
Be sensible. Now, let's go.

1430
01:11:21.720 --> 01:11:23.040
If you tried to...

1431
01:11:23.160 --> 01:11:24.200
What?

1432
01:11:26.400 --> 01:11:28.880
If you looked at it as a friend
rather than...

1433
01:11:30.640 --> 01:11:32.160
If we were as we say we...

1434
01:11:33.520 --> 01:11:35.160
If you were being sober, sensible...

1435
01:11:35.240 --> 01:11:36.680
What are you muttering about?

1436
01:11:38.040 --> 01:11:39.840
There are sacrifices
people make for each other.

1437
01:11:39.960 --> 01:11:41.280
What are you muttering?

1438
01:11:45.320 --> 01:11:47.320
If you were a friend, a true friend,

1439
01:11:48.760 --> 01:11:50.520
you'd say go.

1440
01:11:50.680 --> 01:11:51.760
Stay, I mean.

1441
01:11:53.600 --> 01:11:54.720
You'd set me free.

1442
01:11:58.000 --> 01:11:59.240
Are you that blind?

1443
01:11:59.920 --> 01:12:02.600
- Maybe she's what I need.
- What, your little ingénue?

1444
01:12:04.240 --> 01:12:06.080
Do you know yourself at all?

1445
01:12:06.240 --> 01:12:08.360
You can say that,
but even as I'm talking to you it's like...

1446
01:12:08.520 --> 01:12:09.320
What?

1447
01:12:09.440 --> 01:12:11.640
I'm... I'm not really here,
I'm just going through the...

1448
01:12:12.680 --> 01:12:15.200
You can't explain this away
because I'm just talking and walking

1449
01:12:15.360 --> 01:12:17.200
but in my... in my dreams it's all...

1450
01:12:17.280 --> 01:12:18.320
- It's all her.
- Stop.

1451
01:12:18.440 --> 01:12:20.840
- I can't. It won't. I don't control it.
- Stop it! Stop it now!

1452
01:12:22.200 --> 01:12:23.320
I'm to listen to that?

1453
01:12:23.440 --> 01:12:27.280
You're gonna... talk like this
about another woman, right to me?

1454
01:12:29.360 --> 01:12:30.720
- It hurts.
- Oh, come on.

1455
01:12:30.840 --> 01:12:32.480
This is not...
We're not like that.

1456
01:12:34.200 --> 01:12:35.840
You... you can understand.

1457
01:12:37.440 --> 01:12:38.240
Objectively.

1458
01:12:41.360 --> 01:12:42.640
She's taken over my brain.

1459
01:12:43.880 --> 01:12:47.120
My sweetest dreams, my best...
All the best...

1460
01:12:47.240 --> 01:12:48.400
You have to let me go.

1461
01:12:49.640 --> 01:12:50.800
Just let me go.

1462
01:12:51.960 --> 01:12:52.920
Say you let me go.

1463
01:12:57.040 --> 01:12:58.480
How can you ask that?

1464
01:13:00.600 --> 01:13:02.720
What do you think I am? I'm...

1465
01:13:02.880 --> 01:13:04.160
I'm just a woman.

1466
01:13:06.600 --> 01:13:09.760
I'm just like any other woman,
like the... like the women that you write.

1467
01:13:09.880 --> 01:13:11.520
- No, you're not.
- Like... like the...

1468
01:13:11.640 --> 01:13:12.800
You know you're not.

1469
01:13:13.800 --> 01:13:15.280
We both know that you're not.

1470
01:13:17.400 --> 01:13:18.640
You don't have to be.

1471
01:13:21.320 --> 01:13:22.480
Just understand.

1472
01:13:24.000 --> 01:13:25.440
Try to understand.

1473
01:13:26.400 --> 01:13:29.600
I've never, never felt this before.

1474
01:13:32.040 --> 01:13:33.600
I didn't know this existed.

1475
01:13:35.640 --> 01:13:36.800
It's something...

1476
01:13:38.960 --> 01:13:40.320
...something I've ne...

1477
01:13:44.280 --> 01:13:49.040
Love... is the only thing on this earth
that can make you happy.

1478
01:13:51.560 --> 01:13:52.920
Not... not art.

1479
01:13:55.480 --> 01:13:56.440
Not...

1480
01:13:58.000 --> 01:13:59.520
...whatever else.

1481
01:14:00.840 --> 01:14:02.200
All that...

1482
01:14:05.640 --> 01:14:06.880
There's only love.

1483
01:14:09.200 --> 01:14:12.000
It's music, it's poetry.

1484
01:14:14.480 --> 01:14:15.680
How they write of it.

1485
01:14:17.200 --> 01:14:18.320
It's how I write it.

1486
01:14:20.280 --> 01:14:21.800
How you're meant to love
when you're young

1487
01:14:21.960 --> 01:14:24.280
but I'm at meetings and editors' doors
and publishers' parties

1488
01:14:24.400 --> 01:14:26.200
and now it's here,
beckoning me towards it.

1489
01:14:26.320 --> 01:14:28.600
- You might really have gone mad.
- If I have, does it matter?

1490
01:14:28.720 --> 01:14:31.000
All of you are just ganging up
to torture me today!

1491
01:14:31.120 --> 01:14:32.960
You won't understand.
She doesn't understand.

1492
01:14:33.040 --> 01:14:35.440
She doesn't want to understand.

1493
01:14:35.600 --> 01:14:37.440
Am I really so old,

1494
01:14:39.720 --> 01:14:41.160
and so ugly,

1495
01:14:42.440 --> 01:14:47.440
and so irrelevant that you can talk to me
about other women with no shame?

1496
01:14:51.480 --> 01:14:52.920
I no longer matter.

1497
01:14:55.520 --> 01:14:56.920
I'm no longer here.

1498
01:14:59.160 --> 01:15:01.080
I'm not a real contender.

1499
01:15:03.560 --> 01:15:05.200
I'm not even an obstacle.

1500
01:15:08.480 --> 01:15:10.080
You're just infatuated.

1501
01:15:11.800 --> 01:15:13.520
- I'm...
- I'd die if you left me.

1502
01:15:13.640 --> 01:15:14.600
Oh.

1503
01:15:15.240 --> 01:15:18.600
You're just...
You're mad for some silly little girl.

1504
01:15:18.720 --> 01:15:19.800
You have a crush.

1505
01:15:20.840 --> 01:15:22.200
And she has a crush.

1506
01:15:23.520 --> 01:15:25.440
- You're the last page of my life.
- Someone might come.

1507
01:15:25.560 --> 01:15:27.680
I don't care! I don't care!

1508
01:15:27.760 --> 01:15:28.720
Why would I care?

1509
01:15:28.880 --> 01:15:31.480
I should be embarrassed
that I'm in love with you?

1510
01:15:31.600 --> 01:15:32.720
Please.

1511
01:15:33.480 --> 01:15:35.120
I can't let you do this. I won't...

1512
01:15:35.240 --> 01:15:37.160
I won't let you do this to yourself.

1513
01:15:39.080 --> 01:15:40.160
You're mine.

1514
01:15:42.840 --> 01:15:43.880
Yes?

1515
01:15:46.200 --> 01:15:47.360
Remember.

1516
01:15:48.960 --> 01:15:50.120
You're mine.

1517
01:15:51.280 --> 01:15:53.400
These are my eyes,

1518
01:15:54.840 --> 01:15:56.360
my nose,

1519
01:15:57.720 --> 01:15:59.520
my mouth.

1520
01:16:00.840 --> 01:16:02.400
- You're all mine.
- Please!

1521
01:16:07.120 --> 01:16:09.120
You're the greatest writer alive.

1522
01:16:11.520 --> 01:16:13.280
You're the greatest talent.

1523
01:16:14.280 --> 01:16:16.160
Oh, you're the greatest writer
I've ever known.

1524
01:16:16.240 --> 01:16:17.680
I find it hard to look at you.

1525
01:16:19.040 --> 01:16:24.200
You, your... your characters
are so... alive.

1526
01:16:25.800 --> 01:16:29.920
They have integrity and sincerity,
in every line this weight.

1527
01:16:31.080 --> 01:16:32.360
But it's fresh.

1528
01:16:33.480 --> 01:16:34.520
It's funny.

1529
01:16:36.600 --> 01:16:38.000
Everyone reads you.

1530
01:16:39.080 --> 01:16:40.160
Everyone knows your name.

1531
01:16:40.320 --> 01:16:42.640
And you write books in this world,

1532
01:16:43.600 --> 01:16:45.440
in this age.

1533
01:16:47.600 --> 01:16:50.200
Do you know
how obsolete you should be?

1534
01:16:50.320 --> 01:16:51.480
How antiquated.

1535
01:16:53.640 --> 01:16:55.920
But the excitement,

1536
01:16:56.040 --> 01:16:57.520
the mention of your name...

1537
01:16:57.600 --> 01:16:59.840
The room fizzes at the mention of you.

1538
01:17:00.000 --> 01:17:01.360
And that's you.

1539
01:17:01.440 --> 01:17:02.880
That's your work.

1540
01:17:03.000 --> 01:17:04.920
Your talent, your fame.

1541
01:17:07.600 --> 01:17:09.040
And who could not love it?

1542
01:17:10.440 --> 01:17:11.800
Love you for it?

1543
01:17:13.520 --> 01:17:15.000
I can't blame her.

1544
01:17:17.280 --> 01:17:19.760
But, my love,
could she tell you these things?

1545
01:17:20.920 --> 01:17:23.520
Could you believe them if she did?
Could you trust it? Could you?

1546
01:17:23.640 --> 01:17:24.600
Look at me.

1547
01:17:26.040 --> 01:17:27.000
Look at me.

1548
01:17:30.920 --> 01:17:32.080
Am I lying?

1549
01:17:34.000 --> 01:17:35.720
Have I ever lied to you? Have I?

1550
01:17:39.000 --> 01:17:40.720
I know you.

1551
01:17:42.360 --> 01:17:44.760
Darling, I know you.

1552
01:17:46.680 --> 01:17:48.680
And I tell you the truth.

1553
01:17:50.720 --> 01:17:53.360
And the truth is that you are...

1554
01:17:55.760 --> 01:17:56.760
You...

1555
01:17:58.920 --> 01:18:00.560
...are extraordinary.

1556
01:18:02.400 --> 01:18:04.320
You won't leave me?
You won't really go, will you?

1557
01:18:04.440 --> 01:18:07.120
You... You need me, don't you?
You need me, don't you, darling?

1558
01:18:07.280 --> 01:18:08.640
Come!

1559
01:18:08.760 --> 01:18:11.440
Oh, what are we to do, my love?

1560
01:18:13.440 --> 01:18:15.360
- I'll do as you will.
- Yeah?

1561
01:18:19.560 --> 01:18:21.960
I've never had any will of my own.

1562
01:18:22.080 --> 01:18:24.120
I don't know how you can
find that attractive.

1563
01:18:28.440 --> 01:18:29.960
Are we leaving now, then?

1564
01:18:30.120 --> 01:18:31.720
We can stay longer if you like.

1565
01:18:36.000 --> 01:18:36.960
No.

1566
01:18:37.080 --> 01:18:38.000
If you want to.

1567
01:18:38.120 --> 01:18:39.480
Or I could go ahead.

1568
01:18:39.560 --> 01:18:42.440
You could stay, leave in a few days.
My brother wouldn't mind.

1569
01:18:42.600 --> 01:18:45.240
No, I'll leave with you.
Just take me away from this place.

1570
01:18:45.320 --> 01:18:46.960
If that's what you want.

1571
01:18:47.120 --> 01:18:48.080
Yes.

1572
01:18:48.840 --> 01:18:50.880
Imagine after all that
and we miss the ferry!

1573
01:18:51.000 --> 01:18:52.240
Car's here.

1574
01:18:52.320 --> 01:18:53.400
Shall we go?

1575
01:18:53.480 --> 01:18:55.480
To the car, to the train, to the ferry.

1576
01:18:55.600 --> 01:18:56.560
Change, another car.

1577
01:18:56.640 --> 01:18:58.760
Perhaps even a taxi
if we can't face the train.

1578
01:18:58.880 --> 01:19:01.840
Perhaps even a helicopter
if you miss the ferry, cos it won't wait.

1579
01:19:03.000 --> 01:19:04.240
Even for you.

1580
01:19:06.320 --> 01:19:08.360
And you got to remember
to say hello to my friend.

1581
01:19:08.480 --> 01:19:10.760
You gonna remember?
My friend I told you about.

1582
01:19:11.840 --> 01:19:12.800
Which one?

1583
01:19:14.520 --> 01:19:15.680
I told you.

1584
01:19:17.160 --> 01:19:18.240
The actor.

1585
01:19:19.200 --> 01:19:20.160
From the city.

1586
01:19:22.560 --> 01:19:24.880
You'll run into him
at one of your theatre bars

1587
01:19:24.960 --> 01:19:28.240
or premieres we always see you at.

1588
01:19:28.320 --> 01:19:30.640
My wife cuts the pictures
out the papers, don't you?

1589
01:19:32.280 --> 01:19:34.280
You'll run into him.

1590
01:19:34.440 --> 01:19:35.720
Bound to.

1591
01:19:36.680 --> 01:19:38.680
I told him all about you.

1592
01:19:39.840 --> 01:19:42.240
Paths got to cross
unless he's dead.

1593
01:19:44.160 --> 01:19:46.760
Me and him, we had a night.

1594
01:19:48.120 --> 01:19:50.400
We had a night slash morning.

1595
01:19:52.520 --> 01:19:53.840
Slash lunchtime.

1596
01:19:56.280 --> 01:19:57.800
I seen him in a play in town.

1597
01:19:57.960 --> 01:20:02.120
They were touring here or something.
Play called... What was it called?

1598
01:20:04.120 --> 01:20:05.600
Something about a robbery.

1599
01:20:07.880 --> 01:20:09.920
There was robber in it.

1600
01:20:12.400 --> 01:20:16.320
Very... funny... play.

1601
01:20:19.280 --> 01:20:20.440
And I seen him in the bar

1602
01:20:20.560 --> 01:20:22.160
and I gone up to him
and I said, "Well done."

1603
01:20:22.280 --> 01:20:23.320
And he said, "Thank you."

1604
01:20:23.440 --> 01:20:26.520
And I told him, and it was true,
he'd been extraordinary.

1605
01:20:28.920 --> 01:20:30.160
Extraordinary.

1606
01:20:32.360 --> 01:20:35.520
And when it's live you can tell.

1607
01:20:37.640 --> 01:20:40.440
He was an extraordinary actor.

1608
01:20:42.920 --> 01:20:44.840
I never seen anyone better.

1609
01:20:47.720 --> 01:20:51.000
And he told me this story of this time
when this guy, this other guy,

1610
01:20:51.080 --> 01:20:53.000
friend of my friend,
the actor in the play,

1611
01:20:53.160 --> 01:20:54.720
had this line to say.

1612
01:20:56.160 --> 01:21:00.800
"We've been cornered.
That's a snag."

1613
01:21:02.600 --> 01:21:04.800
And he said, accident,
slip of the tongue or something,

1614
01:21:04.920 --> 01:21:11.160
not, "We've been cornered,
that's a snag", which was the line,

1615
01:21:12.800 --> 01:21:14.600
he said,

1616
01:21:14.760 --> 01:21:16.680
"We've been cornered by a slag."

1617
01:21:21.240 --> 01:21:22.280
By a slag!

1618
01:21:25.840 --> 01:21:27.080
A slag.

1619
01:21:30.640 --> 01:21:32.840
In front of the, like, audience
and everything.

1620
01:21:35.920 --> 01:21:36.960
Slag.

1621
01:21:41.400 --> 01:21:42.920
Theatre went mad apparently.

1622
01:21:45.720 --> 01:21:46.880
Extraordinary.

1623
01:21:47.920 --> 01:21:49.840
We're all so sad to see you go.

1624
01:21:49.920 --> 01:21:51.200
I mean, you're the writer one.

1625
01:21:51.360 --> 01:21:54.640
How would you be if someone did that
in the middle of your play?

1626
01:21:54.720 --> 01:21:56.240
I've never written for the theatre.

1627
01:21:56.360 --> 01:21:59.120
I think you'll find the answer is,
if you use your imagination,

1628
01:21:59.240 --> 01:22:01.440
absolutely fucking furious.

1629
01:22:02.400 --> 01:22:03.680
Are you going to cry?

1630
01:22:04.520 --> 01:22:06.080
Oh, you're the sweetest thing.

1631
01:22:06.240 --> 01:22:07.880
Time's gone by so fast.

1632
01:22:08.840 --> 01:22:10.080
We should try to come and see you.

1633
01:22:10.200 --> 01:22:11.840
Christ. All right.

1634
01:22:13.160 --> 01:22:14.520
Well, me and my Masha, then.

1635
01:22:14.680 --> 01:22:17.840
- You really are the sweetest thing.
- (Petrusha) I'm ready! I'm ready!

1636
01:22:17.960 --> 01:22:19.600
You will not stop me.
We'd better get going.

1637
01:22:19.680 --> 01:22:21.440
- I've just seen the time.
- Are you sure?

1638
01:22:21.520 --> 01:22:22.560
I'll be in the car.

1639
01:22:22.680 --> 01:22:25.080
I've left an envelope
in the kitchen for you.

1640
01:22:25.160 --> 01:22:27.280
Would you split it between everyone?

1641
01:22:27.360 --> 01:22:29.760
The gardener, all that,
and, of course, you two.

1642
01:22:29.880 --> 01:22:32.360
Will do, will do.

1643
01:22:32.480 --> 01:22:33.800
And thank you.

1644
01:22:35.640 --> 01:22:39.000
I know we quarrel, but...
we're good friends really, aren't we?

1645
01:22:44.080 --> 01:22:45.040
The best.

1646
01:22:49.440 --> 01:22:51.640
You will stay in touch this time,
won't you? It's just...

1647
01:22:54.600 --> 01:22:57.720
- Without you...
- I'll try. This time I'll really do it.

1648
01:22:57.800 --> 01:23:00.000
Has anyone seen Kostya?
I must say goodbye.

1649
01:23:00.120 --> 01:23:02.120
You are really very late.

1650
01:23:02.240 --> 01:23:05.400
He was pissing around with the car
and now you're all gonna miss the ferry.

1651
01:23:06.840 --> 01:23:07.880
What?

1652
01:23:10.160 --> 01:23:11.120
What?

1653
01:23:13.160 --> 01:23:14.400
We're going.

1654
01:23:15.360 --> 01:23:16.320
We're going.

1655
01:23:19.120 --> 01:23:20.280
We're going.

1656
01:23:23.320 --> 01:23:24.280
(Arkadina) We're gone!

1657
01:23:30.520 --> 01:23:31.520
(Trigorin) I'm running.

1658
01:23:31.680 --> 01:23:32.640
I'm running.

1659
01:23:32.760 --> 01:23:34.160
I knew that couldn't be it.

1660
01:23:34.280 --> 01:23:35.240
(Trigorin) We're going.

1661
01:23:35.400 --> 01:23:37.160
And I've decided.

1662
01:23:37.280 --> 01:23:39.560
You have? What?

1663
01:23:39.680 --> 01:23:41.120
I'm going to come to the city.

1664
01:23:41.960 --> 01:23:44.080
I'm going to leave this place.

1665
01:23:45.040 --> 01:23:46.680
I'm going be an actress.

1666
01:23:47.640 --> 01:23:48.600
You're coming?

1667
01:23:48.680 --> 01:23:50.720
I won't be here
when they wake up in the morning.

1668
01:23:51.840 --> 01:23:52.800
Call my agent.

1669
01:23:52.920 --> 01:23:53.880
What?

1670
01:23:54.040 --> 01:23:56.360
I'll get him to book you a room
somewhere, a suite even.

1671
01:23:56.480 --> 01:23:58.760
- Just call him and he'll...
- I...

1672
01:23:58.920 --> 01:24:00.320
He'll sort everything out.

1673
01:24:00.400 --> 01:24:03.000
You just go...
You just stay where he tells you to stay, yes?

1674
01:24:03.080 --> 01:24:05.200
All on my tab. Go wild, go crazy.

1675
01:24:07.880 --> 01:24:10.080
Who's your agent?
I don't have his number. I...

1676
01:24:11.120 --> 01:24:12.400
It'll be online somewhere.

1677
01:24:17.000 --> 01:24:18.840
For a minute there
I almost walked away,

1678
01:24:20.360 --> 01:24:22.680
let myself live a life
where I'd never see this face again.

1679
01:24:25.840 --> 01:24:26.800
Your eyes.

1680
01:24:27.680 --> 01:24:29.600
You will. The... the car...

1681
01:24:39.960 --> 01:24:40.920
My angel.

1682
01:25:18.520 --> 01:25:19.520
I'll be seeing you.

1683
01:25:20.160 --> 01:25:21.120
Soon.

1684
01:25:27.280 --> 01:25:28.840
- Soon?
- Yes.

1685
01:25:34.760 --> 01:25:35.720
Yes.

1686
01:26:04.160 --> 01:26:06.160
(Audience applauds)

1687
01:26:11.440 --> 01:26:13.840
(♪ Piano music)

1688
01:29:15.360 --> 01:29:17.480
(Piano music continues)

1689
01:30:01.720 --> 01:30:03.560
(♪ Music stops)

1690
01:30:05.240 --> 01:30:06.320
Konstantin?

1691
01:30:07.840 --> 01:30:08.800
No.

1692
01:30:10.160 --> 01:30:11.440
Konstantin?

1693
01:30:12.200 --> 01:30:14.960
- Not in here.
- I'll go and look.

1694
01:30:15.080 --> 01:30:16.120
Leave it.

1695
01:30:18.320 --> 01:30:19.280
Why?

1696
01:30:19.960 --> 01:30:21.200
It doesn't matter.

1697
01:30:22.280 --> 01:30:23.520
It doesn't matter?

1698
01:30:24.480 --> 01:30:26.520
He gets so fretful without him.

1699
01:30:27.240 --> 01:30:31.400
It's always, "Where's Kostya?
Where's Kostya?"

1700
01:30:31.560 --> 01:30:33.200
Scared of being alone.

1701
01:30:33.320 --> 01:30:34.960
Especially in this weather.

1702
01:30:36.000 --> 01:30:37.560
Did you see the lake?

1703
01:30:39.360 --> 01:30:41.360
There are these huge waves on it.

1704
01:30:41.480 --> 01:30:42.800
I saw.

1705
01:30:42.920 --> 01:30:45.680
Huge waves crashing all across it.

1706
01:30:45.800 --> 01:30:47.440
I saw.

1707
01:30:47.520 --> 01:30:49.520
Like a picture book or something.

1708
01:30:50.560 --> 01:30:52.200
Out there in the dark.

1709
01:30:53.640 --> 01:30:56.080
They should get rid of that stage.

1710
01:30:56.160 --> 01:30:57.800
What?

1711
01:30:57.880 --> 01:31:00.960
It makes a hell of a racket in this wind.

1712
01:31:01.040 --> 01:31:03.840
I don't know
when this weather will let up.

1713
01:31:03.920 --> 01:31:06.360
It's been like this for days now.

1714
01:31:06.520 --> 01:31:09.000
Should have taken it down years ago.

1715
01:31:09.120 --> 01:31:11.520
Like some skeleton from the past.

1716
01:31:11.600 --> 01:31:12.560
Yes.

1717
01:31:13.440 --> 01:31:16.880
I thought I heard someone crying there
when I passed by yesterday.

1718
01:31:17.000 --> 01:31:19.280
(Sarcastically) Oh. Did you really?

1719
01:31:24.200 --> 01:31:25.720
Let's go home, Masha.

1720
01:31:25.880 --> 01:31:27.280
What?

1721
01:31:27.360 --> 01:31:28.800
Let's go home.

1722
01:31:28.880 --> 01:31:30.800
The baby will want you.

1723
01:31:30.920 --> 01:31:34.200
No, I'm going to stay here tonight.

1724
01:31:34.360 --> 01:31:35.800
Come on, don't be silly.

1725
01:31:35.960 --> 01:31:37.320
Your mother can deal with it.

1726
01:31:38.120 --> 01:31:39.080
What?

1727
01:31:39.240 --> 01:31:41.840
Him. Your mother can deal with him.

1728
01:31:42.800 --> 01:31:44.840
That's three days without you.

1729
01:31:44.920 --> 01:31:46.480
He's fine without me.

1730
01:31:47.320 --> 01:31:50.600
(Sarcastically) Baby, baby, baby.

1731
01:31:50.680 --> 01:31:53.080
It's the only word
out your mouth nowadays.

1732
01:31:53.240 --> 01:31:54.360
Just come home.

1733
01:31:54.440 --> 01:31:55.880
- You go home.
- How?

1734
01:31:56.040 --> 01:31:58.280
- What?
- Your father's not back.

1735
01:31:58.360 --> 01:31:59.520
He will be soon

1736
01:31:59.600 --> 01:32:03.160
and then you can ask him for the car
and drive that home, can't you?

1737
01:32:03.320 --> 01:32:05.880
- I'm sure he won't let me.
- Ask him from me.

1738
01:32:07.080 --> 01:32:08.640
You'll come home tomorrow?

1739
01:32:08.800 --> 01:32:11.400
Do you ever give up? Ever?

1740
01:32:11.520 --> 01:32:13.480
Petrusha wants to sleep in here tonight.

1741
01:32:13.600 --> 01:32:16.280
Oh. I'll do that later.

1742
01:32:16.400 --> 01:32:19.280
Old people turn straight back
into children, don't they?

1743
01:32:19.400 --> 01:32:20.880
Well, I'll go see about...

1744
01:32:21.000 --> 01:32:22.120
Yeah, bye.

1745
01:32:25.040 --> 01:32:26.000
Bye.

1746
01:32:27.160 --> 01:32:30.160
Yes. God, I thought you left hours ago.

1747
01:32:33.400 --> 01:32:34.440
And Konstantin...

1748
01:32:37.640 --> 01:32:38.600
Good night.

1749
01:32:49.120 --> 01:32:51.360
Are you onto the next play,
then, yet, Kostya?

1750
01:32:51.440 --> 01:32:53.760
Hm? Or are you still working
on your old one?

1751
01:32:56.040 --> 01:32:57.680
I don't know how he does it,
do you, Masha?

1752
01:33:00.880 --> 01:33:03.160
They mentioned you
in an article the other day.

1753
01:33:04.320 --> 01:33:06.760
We cut it out.
I have it at home if you want it.

1754
01:33:08.320 --> 01:33:12.680
Your name right alongside
all these real writers.

1755
01:33:17.200 --> 01:33:18.160
Oh.

1756
01:33:19.280 --> 01:33:21.400
God, cos you are a real writer too,
I suppose.

1757
01:33:25.120 --> 01:33:27.720
Soon as you make money from writing,
you're a real writer, I think.

1758
01:33:30.120 --> 01:33:31.880
And I thought
there was no money in theatre.

1759
01:33:35.120 --> 01:33:37.360
And you've got all handsome of late.

1760
01:33:41.640 --> 01:33:43.920
- Be a little kinder to my Masha, please.
- Leave him alone.

1761
01:33:44.040 --> 01:33:47.240
All a woman needs
is not to be treated unkindly.

1762
01:33:47.320 --> 01:33:49.520
- Some affection, or at least not...
- He's working.

1763
01:33:49.640 --> 01:33:53.240
You'd be amazed how little is needed
to make the pain so much less.

1764
01:33:54.440 --> 01:33:55.760
Why do you have to do that?

1765
01:33:56.840 --> 01:33:59.920
He wasn't doing anything
and now you've made him angry.

1766
01:34:00.840 --> 01:34:04.120
- I just feel so sorry for you.
- Oh, my God.

1767
01:34:04.240 --> 01:34:05.800
Please, not your pity.

1768
01:34:08.240 --> 01:34:09.200
You know...

1769
01:34:10.440 --> 01:34:12.880
You know, Masha, I understand this.

1770
01:34:12.960 --> 01:34:14.200
Oh, I know.

1771
01:34:14.840 --> 01:34:16.400
I know you've spent your life

1772
01:34:16.520 --> 01:34:20.520
believing in unrequited,
hopeless, romantic love.

1773
01:34:21.600 --> 01:34:23.800
But it's not actually real.

1774
01:34:23.920 --> 01:34:26.000
It doesn't have to be.

1775
01:34:26.120 --> 01:34:29.480
You can either spend your time
playing the victim

1776
01:34:29.640 --> 01:34:33.880
and suffering quietly with ideas
that are only in your own head,

1777
01:34:34.760 --> 01:34:36.640
or you can act like it's the real world.

1778
01:34:37.720 --> 01:34:39.360
You've done the first.

1779
01:34:39.440 --> 01:34:41.080
I'll try the latter.

1780
01:34:41.920 --> 01:34:43.760
The school's closing.

1781
01:34:43.880 --> 01:34:45.760
They'll transfer him.

1782
01:34:45.880 --> 01:34:48.680
I'm going to make him ask
for a job on the mainland.

1783
01:34:49.440 --> 01:34:51.640
We can move.

1784
01:34:51.760 --> 01:34:54.120
And then it will all be gone.

1785
01:34:54.240 --> 01:34:57.640
Rip it out at the root,

1786
01:34:57.760 --> 01:34:59.600
else you lose your life to it.

1787
01:35:00.280 --> 01:35:02.000
(♪ Slow piano playing)

1788
01:35:07.840 --> 01:35:09.320
(Masha sighs)

1789
01:35:09.400 --> 01:35:11.120
Is that Kostya playing that?

1790
01:35:12.280 --> 01:35:14.680
Always this stuff
when he's depressed.

1791
01:35:14.840 --> 01:35:18.680
Just not having him
in front of my eyes all the time.

1792
01:35:19.880 --> 01:35:24.760
When we're out of here,
it will all be gone, won't it?

1793
01:35:26.280 --> 01:35:28.120
I'll have forgotten him in a month. I'll...

1794
01:35:29.680 --> 01:35:30.720
You'll see.

1795
01:35:30.800 --> 01:35:34.560
And now, with inflation,
they've made it impossible to keep up.

1796
01:35:34.640 --> 01:35:37.400
It's just the way of the world,
it's not a conspiracy.

1797
01:35:38.480 --> 01:35:40.480
Easy for you to laugh.

1798
01:35:40.600 --> 01:35:43.280
You've been a doctor
how many years? Twenty?

1799
01:35:43.400 --> 01:35:45.680
- I bet you're nicely set up.
- Oh, you think?

1800
01:35:46.840 --> 01:35:50.160
Thirty years of practice
where I couldn't call my soul my own

1801
01:35:50.280 --> 01:35:53.240
so I decide to see the world,

1802
01:35:53.360 --> 01:35:54.880
reclaim some life,

1803
01:35:56.360 --> 01:36:00.000
and thirty years wiped out
in less than five.

1804
01:36:01.800 --> 01:36:02.880
The coffers are empty.

1805
01:36:02.960 --> 01:36:04.120
So you didn't leave.

1806
01:36:05.000 --> 01:36:07.080
- No car.
- Why do you have to be so useless?

1807
01:36:07.200 --> 01:36:10.080
- He isn't back with the car yet.
- Why can't you just leave me alone?

1808
01:36:10.160 --> 01:36:12.280
So, this is now
Konstantin's study in here?

1809
01:36:12.400 --> 01:36:14.640
With the garden
it's far better for writing.

1810
01:36:14.760 --> 01:36:16.600
He can go outside and think
whenever he likes.

1811
01:36:16.720 --> 01:36:20.080
Where's my sister?
I thought she was in here.

1812
01:36:20.160 --> 01:36:22.160
She went to pick him up from the ferry.

1813
01:36:23.320 --> 01:36:26.200
Why has he come?
I don't need him.

1814
01:36:26.360 --> 01:36:28.040
Your sister wanted him here.

1815
01:36:29.000 --> 01:36:31.200
You told her to come here,

1816
01:36:31.280 --> 01:36:32.880
said I needed her.

1817
01:36:33.880 --> 01:36:35.720
Then she said she needed him.

1818
01:36:37.640 --> 01:36:39.760
I must be seriously ill for that.

1819
01:36:42.320 --> 01:36:46.040
Funny to be seriously ill
and not to be given any medication.

1820
01:36:46.160 --> 01:36:47.920
Would you like a paracetamol?

1821
01:36:49.640 --> 01:36:50.960
Where's Kostya?

1822
01:36:52.200 --> 01:36:54.920
I want to give him an idea
for one of those plays of his.

1823
01:36:56.560 --> 01:36:58.520
"The Man Who Wanted."

1824
01:37:00.360 --> 01:37:01.480
Like me.

1825
01:37:04.040 --> 01:37:05.880
Because I wanted to be a writer.

1826
01:37:07.560 --> 01:37:08.800
Never was one.

1827
01:37:10.560 --> 01:37:12.480
Wanted to be a great speaker.

1828
01:37:13.400 --> 01:37:14.840
Was a terrible one.

1829
01:37:16.400 --> 01:37:18.800
I'd sum up
till the sweat poured off my face,

1830
01:37:18.920 --> 01:37:21.160
but still couldn't seem to find the point.

1831
01:37:23.880 --> 01:37:25.200
Wanted to get married.

1832
01:37:26.600 --> 01:37:27.640
Never did.

1833
01:37:30.440 --> 01:37:32.200
Wanted to live in the city.

1834
01:37:33.200 --> 01:37:36.200
And here I am,
dying in the country.

1835
01:37:37.240 --> 01:37:39.160
Wanted to be a High Court judge
and became one.

1836
01:37:39.320 --> 01:37:41.440
I didn't want that.
That just happened.

1837
01:37:42.800 --> 01:37:44.600
How very magnanimous,
Your Honour.

1838
01:37:45.600 --> 01:37:47.880
Am I not allowed to want more
out of my life?

1839
01:37:48.000 --> 01:37:51.760
Well, it's just a little late in the day
to be having these kinds of revelations.

1840
01:37:51.920 --> 01:37:55.720
I mean, why start complaining
about the life you've lived now?

1841
01:37:55.840 --> 01:37:58.120
It makes no sense.
What can you do? You've lived it.

1842
01:37:58.240 --> 01:37:59.720
And I still want to live it.

1843
01:38:00.680 --> 01:38:03.280
You don't have to be
so deliberately obtuse.

1844
01:38:03.440 --> 01:38:06.320
- You're never ready to die.
- And yet we all must.

1845
01:38:06.440 --> 01:38:08.520
The argument of a happy man.

1846
01:38:09.880 --> 01:38:11.320
And a healthy one.

1847
01:38:12.880 --> 01:38:16.280
But even you will be scared
when it's your turn, Doctor, believe me.

1848
01:38:16.400 --> 01:38:19.960
Well, if I am, I'll work a little harder
to repress it than you are at least.

1849
01:38:20.840 --> 01:38:23.280
Because it's irrational.

1850
01:38:25.040 --> 01:38:26.160
It's animal.

1851
01:38:27.360 --> 01:38:30.040
There's only reason to fear death,
and that's if you believe you're going to hell.

1852
01:38:30.160 --> 01:38:31.120
- Do you?
- No.

1853
01:38:31.200 --> 01:38:32.960
No, exactly.

1854
01:38:33.120 --> 01:38:34.840
You don't believe in God.

1855
01:38:34.960 --> 01:38:36.240
And if you decide to start that up

1856
01:38:36.360 --> 01:38:40.080
as part of this decades-overdue
existential crisis,

1857
01:38:40.200 --> 01:38:41.440
hell won't have you.

1858
01:38:42.320 --> 01:38:44.640
Forty years in the Justice Department.

1859
01:38:44.720 --> 01:38:47.040
What sins have you committed?

1860
01:38:47.120 --> 01:38:48.080
Huh.

1861
01:38:48.200 --> 01:38:49.480
Forty-five years.

1862
01:38:49.640 --> 01:38:50.760
(Chuckles)

1863
01:38:52.520 --> 01:38:53.600
Yes.

1864
01:38:55.960 --> 01:38:57.360
Are we stopping you from working?

1865
01:38:57.480 --> 01:38:58.920
No, no, it's fine.

1866
01:38:59.040 --> 01:39:01.160
You know,
I wanted to ask you, Doctor,

1867
01:39:01.240 --> 01:39:02.920
on this big holiday of yours...

1868
01:39:03.840 --> 01:39:05.080
I was travelling.

1869
01:39:06.040 --> 01:39:07.480
What was the best place?

1870
01:39:08.600 --> 01:39:09.880
Out of everywhere.

1871
01:39:10.640 --> 01:39:12.080
Best place.

1872
01:39:18.120 --> 01:39:19.240
Genoa.

1873
01:39:20.360 --> 01:39:21.400
Why?

1874
01:39:21.520 --> 01:39:22.520
Italy.

1875
01:39:24.880 --> 01:39:26.320
There was this one day.

1876
01:39:28.800 --> 01:39:31.760
There was this... crowd.

1877
01:39:32.640 --> 01:39:33.720
It was, uh...

1878
01:39:36.440 --> 01:39:38.280
You went out of a building
into the street

1879
01:39:38.400 --> 01:39:40.040
and then...

1880
01:39:40.120 --> 01:39:42.000
you were just carried along with it.

1881
01:39:44.520 --> 01:39:48.200
But not pushing
or shoving or struggling.

1882
01:39:48.280 --> 01:39:52.320
You... you were just at one
with everyone.

1883
01:39:54.720 --> 01:39:56.720
It made me think
of your first play, Konstantin.

1884
01:39:56.840 --> 01:39:58.400
- Sorry?
- Oh, you remember.

1885
01:39:58.560 --> 01:40:00.480
Well, maybe wasn't your first.

1886
01:40:00.960 --> 01:40:02.320
First one I saw.

1887
01:40:03.440 --> 01:40:05.240
I saw the stage still up by the lake.

1888
01:40:06.040 --> 01:40:09.160
And you used that... phrase.

1889
01:40:10.840 --> 01:40:14.640
About collectiveness,
the... universal...

1890
01:40:17.080 --> 01:40:18.520
Unive... Don't you remember?

1891
01:40:23.800 --> 01:40:25.880
The universal soul.

1892
01:40:29.000 --> 01:40:30.440
In that crowd

1893
01:40:31.760 --> 01:40:34.760
I was one person making my way
amongst all these other people.

1894
01:40:37.240 --> 01:40:38.680
And it wasn't just me.

1895
01:40:40.800 --> 01:40:42.280
And it wasn't just them.

1896
01:40:45.680 --> 01:40:47.480
We were this one organism...

1897
01:40:50.600 --> 01:40:52.080
...with one mind.

1898
01:40:58.640 --> 01:41:02.280
It's enough to make you believe
in that universal soul.

1899
01:41:04.880 --> 01:41:09.320
That is what you wrote, isn't it?
In that play you and Nina did.

1900
01:41:10.040 --> 01:41:11.440
I...

1901
01:41:11.520 --> 01:41:13.400
And speaking of Nina...

1902
01:41:14.320 --> 01:41:17.360
Nina... I mean,
what's happened there?

1903
01:41:17.480 --> 01:41:20.000
- I've heard strange things.
- Then you have heard what happened.

1904
01:41:20.160 --> 01:41:22.480
I've heard a thousand versions.
I'd prefer to know the truth.

1905
01:41:22.560 --> 01:41:24.800
- It's a long story.
- Make it a short one, then.

1906
01:41:26.360 --> 01:41:31.400
Sounds like a rather unusual life
she's leading to say the least.

1907
01:41:33.320 --> 01:41:35.520
Tell me the facts, before he arrives.

1908
01:41:39.440 --> 01:41:40.600
She ran away.

1909
01:41:42.440 --> 01:41:45.040
- She went and lived with Boris Trigorin.
- I know that much.

1910
01:41:46.640 --> 01:41:48.280
- She had a baby.
- Yes.

1911
01:41:49.320 --> 01:41:50.480
The baby died.

1912
01:42:00.080 --> 01:42:01.800
And so he left the new life

1913
01:42:03.440 --> 01:42:05.240
and went straight back to the old one.

1914
01:42:07.000 --> 01:42:08.240
Understandably.

1915
01:42:09.400 --> 01:42:10.640
Inevitably.

1916
01:42:12.480 --> 01:42:14.280
He never really left the old one, I think.

1917
01:42:15.840 --> 01:42:18.680
When you don't have a spine,
you can bend both ways.

1918
01:42:21.320 --> 01:42:25.400
Nina's private life
hasn't been very successful.

1919
01:42:26.560 --> 01:42:27.640
And as an actress?

1920
01:42:28.600 --> 01:42:30.320
She started well, but...

1921
01:42:30.440 --> 01:42:33.960
the theatres got smaller,
emptier, further away.

1922
01:42:35.240 --> 01:42:37.240
She had some big roles
at one time, but...

1923
01:42:37.400 --> 01:42:38.800
Why did they stop?

1924
01:42:38.880 --> 01:42:40.440
Are you asking if she was good?

1925
01:42:40.600 --> 01:42:42.920
If that's the answer to the question.

1926
01:42:43.000 --> 01:42:44.800
She always did what was required.

1927
01:42:45.400 --> 01:42:49.240
She'd shout, or cry,
or wave her hands around.

1928
01:42:50.960 --> 01:42:52.600
She was most definitely acting.

1929
01:42:54.920 --> 01:42:56.720
But there were these moments,

1930
01:42:57.800 --> 01:42:58.760
just a moment,

1931
01:43:00.200 --> 01:43:03.920
in a line or... in a final breath.

1932
01:43:05.280 --> 01:43:06.360
So, she was talented?

1933
01:43:06.520 --> 01:43:07.960
I don't... I can't say.

1934
01:43:08.080 --> 01:43:09.880
What would she say?

1935
01:43:10.040 --> 01:43:11.400
I never saw her.

1936
01:43:12.760 --> 01:43:14.000
She wouldn't let me.

1937
01:43:15.360 --> 01:43:18.320
Wherever she stayed,
she'd have someone say she wasn't in.

1938
01:43:18.440 --> 01:43:20.720
Or I had the wrong place.

1939
01:43:20.840 --> 01:43:22.840
Every stage door said I'd missed her.

1940
01:43:24.360 --> 01:43:25.520
I stopped trying.

1941
01:43:27.440 --> 01:43:28.680
I understood.

1942
01:43:29.200 --> 01:43:32.520
So no word from her, huh?
All this time.

1943
01:43:32.640 --> 01:43:34.240
- She wrote to me.
- She wrote?

1944
01:43:34.960 --> 01:43:36.200
- Letters?
- Yes.

1945
01:43:37.160 --> 01:43:39.040
I suppose it seemed romantic to her.

1946
01:43:39.920 --> 01:43:41.040
She wrote a lot.

1947
01:43:41.960 --> 01:43:42.920
They were...

1948
01:43:44.440 --> 01:43:45.400
...nice.

1949
01:43:46.840 --> 01:43:47.800
They were funny.

1950
01:43:50.560 --> 01:43:51.560
Kind.

1951
01:43:53.960 --> 01:43:55.480
She never complained, but...

1952
01:43:56.600 --> 01:43:58.920
she might as well
have written in her tears.

1953
01:44:00.760 --> 01:44:02.960
Even if she never said
that she was unhappy.

1954
01:44:05.760 --> 01:44:07.480
Used to sign herself...

1955
01:44:09.800 --> 01:44:10.840
..."The Seagull".

1956
01:44:12.440 --> 01:44:13.440
Why?

1957
01:44:13.520 --> 01:44:14.600
I wouldn't know.

1958
01:44:15.440 --> 01:44:17.640
- She's here now.
- What do you mean, she's here?

1959
01:44:17.760 --> 01:44:20.440
She's been less than a mile away
for almost a week now.

1960
01:44:20.600 --> 01:44:21.840
At her father's?

1961
01:44:21.960 --> 01:44:22.920
Him?

1962
01:44:23.680 --> 01:44:25.840
I don't think
he'd even let her on the driveway.

1963
01:44:26.600 --> 01:44:29.040
They haven't spoken since she left.

1964
01:44:29.840 --> 01:44:31.280
Spoke about her enough.

1965
01:44:31.960 --> 01:44:33.520
She must know
not to go back there.

1966
01:44:34.520 --> 01:44:37.040
She's in town but won't see anyone.
Masha tried, didn't you?

1967
01:44:37.160 --> 01:44:39.680
- Yes, I went.
- But wouldn't even lay eyes on her.

1968
01:44:42.120 --> 01:44:43.440
You did, though, didn't you?

1969
01:44:44.920 --> 01:44:47.120
Yeah. Near here.

1970
01:44:48.000 --> 01:44:50.680
I told her I'm sure
she'd be welcome to visit,

1971
01:44:51.240 --> 01:44:53.280
or come pay us a visit at ours.

1972
01:44:54.440 --> 01:44:55.560
Meet the baby.

1973
01:44:58.840 --> 01:45:02.960
I mean... she did say yes.
She did say she'd come.

1974
01:45:03.080 --> 01:45:04.720
- She won't.
- She was a nice girl.

1975
01:45:04.800 --> 01:45:05.760
What?

1976
01:45:07.200 --> 01:45:08.560
A very nice girl.

1977
01:45:10.240 --> 01:45:11.320
It's a shame...

1978
01:45:12.440 --> 01:45:13.600
...all that happened.

1979
01:45:15.920 --> 01:45:18.640
And I, Chief Justice Sorin,

1980
01:45:20.920 --> 01:45:22.160
was in love with her.

1981
01:45:23.240 --> 01:45:24.400
You old dog.

1982
01:45:28.120 --> 01:45:29.360
Just a little.

1983
01:45:30.720 --> 01:45:31.720
(Laughs)

1984
01:45:31.840 --> 01:45:33.520
- That's them back.
- Yes.

1985
01:45:34.440 --> 01:45:36.280
I don't understand it.

1986
01:45:37.040 --> 01:45:38.760
Time's going on.

1987
01:45:38.880 --> 01:45:41.040
We're all out here, ageing.

1988
01:45:41.160 --> 01:45:42.600
Even he looks older.

1989
01:45:43.560 --> 01:45:47.480
And you're the only one,
only one that's not changed.

1990
01:45:47.600 --> 01:45:49.680
Oh, stop before you jinx me.

1991
01:45:51.520 --> 01:45:53.240
(Trigorin sighs deeply)

1992
01:45:55.080 --> 01:45:56.080
Hello.

1993
01:45:57.320 --> 01:45:58.960
What's all this
about you being ill again?

1994
01:45:59.040 --> 01:46:00.040
(Scoffs)

1995
01:46:01.920 --> 01:46:02.920
I know.

1996
01:46:03.920 --> 01:46:05.240
That's no good, now, is it?

1997
01:46:13.440 --> 01:46:14.840
Masha, hello.

1998
01:46:14.960 --> 01:46:17.960
I... I didn't think
you'd remember me.

1999
01:46:18.720 --> 01:46:21.480
No, I just... Your father here,
he says you're married.

2000
01:46:21.600 --> 01:46:22.640
A while now.

2001
01:46:27.920 --> 01:46:31.160
I thought we could play a game,
like in the old days, hey?

2002
01:46:31.280 --> 01:46:32.440
Hey, Chief Justice?

2003
01:46:32.560 --> 01:46:34.640
Oh, yes, you clever girl.
Let's do that.

2004
01:46:34.760 --> 01:46:36.080
Your mother tells me that we...

2005
01:46:36.960 --> 01:46:38.320
We're...

2006
01:46:45.680 --> 01:46:46.720
And all that.

2007
01:46:47.720 --> 01:46:50.640
Boris found a magazine on the ferry
with a review of yours.

2008
01:46:50.760 --> 01:46:52.000
He saved it for you.

2009
01:46:52.120 --> 01:46:53.840
- Thank you.
- It will make you laugh.

2010
01:46:53.960 --> 01:46:57.040
- The review?
- They all love you, you know. In the city.

2011
01:46:57.800 --> 01:47:01.720
I'm asked about you all the time.
Every bloody journalist I meet.

2012
01:47:02.480 --> 01:47:04.000
You're a one-man cult.

2013
01:47:04.880 --> 01:47:08.520
"Why doesn't he do any interviews?
Why are there no proper photos?"

2014
01:47:08.640 --> 01:47:09.840
I just tell them you're shy.

2015
01:47:10.760 --> 01:47:12.080
They all think you're old, you know.

2016
01:47:13.240 --> 01:47:15.560
Well, no, not old, but...

2017
01:47:15.640 --> 01:47:16.760
older than you are.

2018
01:47:16.880 --> 01:47:19.200
- Do they?
- It's very clever publicity.

2019
01:47:19.880 --> 01:47:22.160
You're the Man in the Iron Mask,

2020
01:47:22.280 --> 01:47:23.480
like, um...

2021
01:47:23.600 --> 01:47:25.920
like... like Batman.

2022
01:47:27.280 --> 01:47:28.800
Complete, complete mystery.

2023
01:47:28.880 --> 01:47:31.480
- When are you going back?
- Tomorrow, I think.

2024
01:47:31.600 --> 01:47:33.280
This is actually a little out of my way.

2025
01:47:33.400 --> 01:47:35.040
I have a literary festival
I'm speaking at.

2026
01:47:35.120 --> 01:47:36.080
Yes?

2027
01:47:38.560 --> 01:47:40.120
Yes.

2028
01:47:40.280 --> 01:47:42.760
And I've a proof to finish
and something for an anthology.

2029
01:47:45.000 --> 01:47:46.040
Same as ever.

2030
01:47:48.960 --> 01:47:50.200
Can't fish in this weather.

2031
01:47:51.360 --> 01:47:52.400
What, sweetheart?

2032
01:47:52.520 --> 01:47:53.920
Can't... can't fish.

2033
01:47:55.360 --> 01:47:57.560
Hope it gets better before tomorrow
before I leave.

2034
01:47:58.560 --> 01:48:00.440
Be a shame not to get any fishing done.

2035
01:48:01.520 --> 01:48:05.160
And that lake...
I need to see that spot again.

2036
01:48:05.280 --> 01:48:06.640
Do you know
what I wanna do, Kostya?

2037
01:48:10.040 --> 01:48:11.040
Konstantin?

2038
01:48:12.880 --> 01:48:14.160
What?

2039
01:48:14.280 --> 01:48:18.520
Your debut play?
You had that little stage up by the lake.

2040
01:48:22.160 --> 01:48:23.480
I want to see that spot again.

2041
01:48:23.600 --> 01:48:25.520
- We were just talking about that.
- Why?

2042
01:48:25.640 --> 01:48:26.680
- Hm?
- Why?

2043
01:48:28.320 --> 01:48:29.800
Idea, story.

2044
01:48:31.360 --> 01:48:32.560
I want to refresh my memory.

2045
01:48:32.640 --> 01:48:35.120
Can you either drive him
or please let him have the car?

2046
01:48:36.880 --> 01:48:38.640
Please let him have the car?

2047
01:48:38.760 --> 01:48:40.560
And how am I meant to get home,
if he's got it?

2048
01:48:40.680 --> 01:48:42.800
Borrow the Land Rover. Or let him.

2049
01:48:42.920 --> 01:48:44.520
He's not insured.

2050
01:48:44.640 --> 01:48:47.320
Look, I'll walk.

2051
01:48:47.480 --> 01:48:49.400
Walk? In this weather?

2052
01:48:50.120 --> 01:48:51.280
It's not so far.

2053
01:48:52.600 --> 01:48:54.520
I used to walk it every day.

2054
01:48:57.320 --> 01:48:59.080
Walk here to see you, didn't I?

2055
01:49:03.160 --> 01:49:04.120
Right.

2056
01:49:05.840 --> 01:49:06.920
Bye.

2057
01:49:11.200 --> 01:49:12.920
Sorry, everyone, it's just...

2058
01:49:14.480 --> 01:49:15.640
The baby...

2059
01:49:21.480 --> 01:49:22.800
I better get going.

2060
01:49:25.840 --> 01:49:27.160
Have a good night.

2061
01:49:33.560 --> 01:49:34.640
Bye, all.

2062
01:49:41.160 --> 01:49:42.280
- Bye.
- It's not my job

2063
01:49:42.400 --> 01:49:45.120
to drive your husband about
or provide his transportation.

2064
01:49:46.040 --> 01:49:47.280
Won't do him any harm to walk it.

2065
01:49:47.400 --> 01:49:49.240
Come on, shall we play?

2066
01:49:49.360 --> 01:49:52.080
It's a ritual on nights like this,
isn't it, Petrusha?

2067
01:49:52.200 --> 01:49:53.920
You have no choice I'm afraid.

2068
01:49:55.280 --> 01:49:57.880
Kostya, did Boris give you
the magazine he found on the ferry?

2069
01:49:58.000 --> 01:49:59.240
I've got one already.

2070
01:49:59.320 --> 01:50:01.240
- Are you playing?
- No, I'm fine.

2071
01:50:01.360 --> 01:50:02.960
Three words. Television.

2072
01:50:03.080 --> 01:50:04.520
Oh, I won't know any telly!

2073
01:50:04.600 --> 01:50:05.640
I've got a good one.

2074
01:50:06.320 --> 01:50:07.280
Book.

2075
01:50:08.720 --> 01:50:11.200
Lots. Many. Ten.

2076
01:50:11.320 --> 01:50:13.240
Ten with... Ten, zero.

2077
01:50:13.360 --> 01:50:14.880
Ten followed by a zero. Hundred.

2078
01:50:18.160 --> 01:50:19.680
"The Old Man and the Sea".

2079
01:50:19.760 --> 01:50:22.040
No, the other thing
you're always complaining about.

2080
01:50:22.160 --> 01:50:23.600
Can't be here all night.

2081
01:50:23.720 --> 01:50:25.320
- Hundred, then something, then...
- No talking.

2082
01:50:25.440 --> 01:50:27.160
- "One Hundred Years of Solitude".
- Yes!

2083
01:50:27.280 --> 01:50:31.360
- "Twelve Angry Men".
- No, he got it. He got it.

2084
01:50:31.480 --> 01:50:32.560
Oh, do you know
what I should have done?

2085
01:50:32.640 --> 01:50:34.840
I should have brought
some of my flowers.

2086
01:50:35.000 --> 01:50:37.240
- Book.
- Oh, not all books. Please.

2087
01:50:37.400 --> 01:50:39.080
Come on. Film. Right.

2088
01:50:39.840 --> 01:50:41.440
Ten. Two.

2089
01:50:41.560 --> 01:50:44.040
- Twelve.
- They're just going to die at home.

2090
01:50:44.160 --> 01:50:46.160
And these lovely students,
they invited me

2091
01:50:46.280 --> 01:50:48.240
to the little theatre at their university

2092
01:50:48.360 --> 01:50:52.800
and they gave me
two enormous bouquets.

2093
01:50:52.880 --> 01:50:54.760
It was actually a little embarrassing.

2094
01:50:55.880 --> 01:50:57.200
What? What? What's that?

2095
01:50:57.320 --> 01:50:58.560
Man. Man.

2096
01:50:58.640 --> 01:51:00.360
Husband. Father.

2097
01:51:00.480 --> 01:51:01.600
- Man.
- Me? What?

2098
01:51:01.720 --> 01:51:04.600
Because, God knows,
students have no money.

2099
01:51:04.720 --> 01:51:08.560
And I'd got these dresses on loan,
which were gorgeous,

2100
01:51:08.640 --> 01:51:10.800
but, goodness, it must have looked
like I was flaunting it

2101
01:51:10.960 --> 01:51:14.480
as they were being so generous
with their few last pennies,

2102
01:51:14.600 --> 01:51:16.880
scraped together for me!

2103
01:51:17.000 --> 01:51:18.520
Go to the next word, next word.

2104
01:51:18.680 --> 01:51:19.680
Twelve something what?

2105
01:51:19.760 --> 01:51:20.920
"Twelve Angry Men".

2106
01:51:23.600 --> 01:51:24.840
We had that already.

2107
01:51:25.640 --> 01:51:28.360
I'm not the only man here.
Why are you pointing at me for?

2108
01:51:29.840 --> 01:51:33.800
He's a man, he's a man, you're a man.

2109
01:51:33.880 --> 01:51:35.440
"Twelve Angry Men".

2110
01:51:35.520 --> 01:51:36.920
You were the second word.

2111
01:51:37.920 --> 01:51:39.920
(♪ Slow piano playing)

2112
01:51:44.040 --> 01:51:45.560
He's depressed again.

2113
01:51:46.760 --> 01:51:49.320
- The music is always a sign.
- It's the reviews.

2114
01:51:51.640 --> 01:51:54.160
- You going?
- I don't read my reviews.

2115
01:51:54.240 --> 01:51:56.600
I often get asked if he's all right.

2116
01:51:57.520 --> 01:51:59.720
His plays have
this kind of bipolar quality.

2117
01:51:59.800 --> 01:52:02.000
I thought they always asked
if he was on drugs.

2118
01:52:02.120 --> 01:52:03.360
I mean, he doesn't help himself.

2119
01:52:03.440 --> 01:52:06.120
He's never written a character
that's even alive.

2120
01:52:06.240 --> 01:52:07.720
We're waiting on you here.

2121
01:52:07.840 --> 01:52:09.120
Petrusha?

2122
01:52:13.640 --> 01:52:14.680
Petrusha?

2123
01:52:17.080 --> 01:52:18.920
Oh!

2124
01:52:19.000 --> 01:52:20.360
He's asleep.

2125
01:52:20.440 --> 01:52:22.440
Can you do something
we all know, please?

2126
01:52:22.560 --> 01:52:24.080
The Supreme Justice is asleep.

2127
01:52:24.200 --> 01:52:27.080
I can't think of one.
You had one. Do yours.

2128
01:52:27.240 --> 01:52:29.360
If I'd lived in a place like this
I'd never have written,

2129
01:52:29.480 --> 01:52:31.160
I'd have just fished.

2130
01:52:31.280 --> 01:52:33.280
- Uh, play.
- Oh, I don't know any plays.

2131
01:52:33.400 --> 01:52:35.000
- One of Konstantin's.
- One word.

2132
01:52:35.120 --> 01:52:36.720
Definitely not one of Konstantin's.

2133
01:52:36.840 --> 01:52:38.200
No, I think he's very good, actually.

2134
01:52:38.320 --> 01:52:40.400
- He has something.
- Who? What, my son?

2135
01:52:40.520 --> 01:52:43.000
It's true, it's more effect than meaning.

2136
01:52:43.160 --> 01:52:45.640
But there's something in effect.
Makes you feel something.

2137
01:52:45.760 --> 01:52:47.320
You always remember it.

2138
01:52:47.440 --> 01:52:49.240
You know, I feel bad to admit it,

2139
01:52:49.360 --> 01:52:51.240
but he does already know,

2140
01:52:51.360 --> 01:52:53.560
I've never seen one of his plays.

2141
01:52:54.440 --> 01:52:55.480
It's a running joke now

2142
01:52:55.600 --> 01:52:58.280
how they're always on
when I'm away or working.

2143
01:52:58.360 --> 01:53:00.240
And they're such short runs.

2144
01:53:00.360 --> 01:53:05.080
You know, as an actor, you actually
never have the chance to go to the theatre.

2145
01:53:05.240 --> 01:53:07.240
I think I see less theatre
than a civilian does.

2146
01:53:07.360 --> 01:53:08.880
Yeah. That's irony for you.

2147
01:53:09.000 --> 01:53:10.800
- Is it?
- Isn't it just?

2148
01:53:10.920 --> 01:53:11.800
Ear.

2149
01:53:11.920 --> 01:53:13.520
- Sounds like, sorry.
- First syllable.

2150
01:53:13.640 --> 01:53:15.160
- No talking.
- (Snorts)

2151
01:53:15.320 --> 01:53:17.360
Pig. You can't do noises.

2152
01:53:17.480 --> 01:53:19.840
Died. Dead. Dead. Dead pig.

2153
01:53:19.960 --> 01:53:21.120
- Hamlet.
- Yes!

2154
01:53:21.200 --> 01:53:22.160
Jesus!

2155
01:53:25.520 --> 01:53:26.600
Fucking extraordinary!

2156
01:53:27.720 --> 01:53:30.200
How do go straight
from dead pig to Hamlet?

2157
01:53:32.440 --> 01:53:33.400
Ham.

2158
01:53:36.480 --> 01:53:38.600
So you three all have a point.

2159
01:53:38.680 --> 01:53:41.000
Well, point for doing them too.
I have a point.

2160
01:53:41.080 --> 01:53:43.760
- Everyone's on one point.
- No, two points.

2161
01:53:43.880 --> 01:53:47.040
- No points.
- It's a bloody rigged game, though.

2162
01:53:47.200 --> 01:53:50.680
Up against book writers and actresses
when it's all books and acting.

2163
01:53:53.760 --> 01:53:55.520
(♪ Piano playing continues faintly)

2164
01:53:58.040 --> 01:54:01.920
We've still got that... thing of yours,
by the way.

2165
01:54:02.000 --> 01:54:04.040
We allowed to do songs?

2166
01:54:04.120 --> 01:54:05.200
You.

2167
01:54:05.280 --> 01:54:06.560
Your thing.

2168
01:54:07.360 --> 01:54:09.000
Me? What thing?

2169
01:54:09.120 --> 01:54:11.120
That bird he shot years ago.

2170
01:54:11.240 --> 01:54:12.480
Who shot a bird?

2171
01:54:14.000 --> 01:54:19.080
We had it stuffed like you asked
and never heard another thing about it.

2172
01:54:19.200 --> 01:54:23.120
So, now you're here, finally,
if you'd like to claim the thing...

2173
01:54:23.240 --> 01:54:24.560
A stuffed what?

2174
01:54:24.680 --> 01:54:26.680
- The seagull Konstantin shot.
- A seagull?

2175
01:54:26.840 --> 01:54:29.440
I asked you to stuff it?
I don't remember this at all.

2176
01:54:29.560 --> 01:54:33.080
I kept asking,
"What am I meant to do with the thing?"

2177
01:54:33.880 --> 01:54:36.400
I asked someone to get in contact
and ask you, but...

2178
01:54:38.200 --> 01:54:40.880
I suppose there were
other things going on, but...

2179
01:54:41.000 --> 01:54:44.120
- We've got this thing up at the house.
- It's not at the house, it's here.

2180
01:54:44.240 --> 01:54:46.280
- It's in the attic Polina.
- It's not, it's here.

2181
01:54:46.360 --> 01:54:49.480
- It's in the attic, by the storage heater.
- It's here, at the top of the pantry.

2182
01:54:49.600 --> 01:54:51.120
- It is not, it is.
- It is here.

2183
01:54:51.240 --> 01:54:53.360
I have no idea
what anyone's talking about.

2184
01:54:53.960 --> 01:54:57.120
That bloody taxidermy gull
in my larder.

2185
01:54:58.360 --> 01:55:01.680
- When did you arise, sir?
- Why did you want Kostya's bird stuffed?

2186
01:55:01.800 --> 01:55:03.800
I haven't got a clue.
I don't remember any of this.

2187
01:55:03.920 --> 01:55:05.640
He had my rifle off me.

2188
01:55:05.760 --> 01:55:07.680
I told you not to bring guns
into the house.

2189
01:55:07.760 --> 01:55:09.840
It's just for the squirrels. Don't fuss.

2190
01:55:09.960 --> 01:55:11.760
You must be a good shot
if you can get them from here.

2191
01:55:11.880 --> 01:55:13.520
- How many words?
- (♪ Music stops abruptly)

2192
01:55:13.640 --> 01:55:14.760
Are we in the way, darling?

2193
01:55:14.880 --> 01:55:18.240
Should we all relocate,
get out of the proper playwright's way.

2194
01:55:18.320 --> 01:55:19.280
Eleven.

2195
01:55:19.400 --> 01:55:21.000
Eleven? Words or syllables?

2196
01:55:21.120 --> 01:55:23.400
I would do anything for love
but I won't do that.

2197
01:55:25.040 --> 01:55:26.760
You have all the luck.

2198
01:55:27.920 --> 01:55:29.160
Your lips were moving.

2199
01:55:29.920 --> 01:55:31.920
Secret skills and all the luck, this man.

2200
01:55:32.440 --> 01:55:34.280
I read it, the review.

2201
01:55:34.360 --> 01:55:35.480
It was funny, wasn't it?

2202
01:55:35.600 --> 01:55:37.440
No need to look quite so mortified.

2203
01:55:37.520 --> 01:55:38.480
Was it good?

2204
01:55:38.600 --> 01:55:40.120
Supportive of you, I thought.

2205
01:55:40.280 --> 01:55:43.280
"The play's not
without its successes, though.

2206
01:55:43.400 --> 01:55:46.440
"An elegant evocation of a lunar night

2207
01:55:46.560 --> 01:55:51.640
"with dying piano chords
and the glimmering starlight

2208
01:55:51.800 --> 01:55:56.440
"would not be out of place
in a Boris Trigorin novel."

2209
01:56:06.040 --> 01:56:07.280
Hello?

2210
01:56:10.240 --> 01:56:11.200
Is...?

2211
01:56:14.120 --> 01:56:15.080
Hello?

2212
01:56:15.920 --> 01:56:17.440
- Nina, Nina, come back!
- No, it's OK.

2213
01:56:17.560 --> 01:56:18.880
- No, come in, come in.
- I shouldn't.

2214
01:56:19.000 --> 01:56:20.160
Come in.

2215
01:56:22.160 --> 01:56:23.440
God, it's you.

2216
01:56:24.400 --> 01:56:25.520
It's really you.

2217
01:56:26.880 --> 01:56:29.400
I've been hoping all day,
hoping all day you'd come.

2218
01:56:30.320 --> 01:56:33.040
My heart kept telling me you were,
but my head wouldn't believe it.

2219
01:56:33.120 --> 01:56:35.440
And... you've come.

2220
01:56:36.960 --> 01:56:38.680
My heart's never the one in the right.

2221
01:56:38.800 --> 01:56:40.040
- Are we alone?
- Yes.

2222
01:56:40.120 --> 01:56:41.240
- Are we?
- It's fine.

2223
01:56:41.360 --> 01:56:42.640
- People are here.
- No one's here.

2224
01:56:42.720 --> 01:56:43.880
Your mother is here.

2225
01:56:48.280 --> 01:56:50.080
This room has changed.

2226
01:56:50.200 --> 01:56:51.920
- It used to be...
- I know.

2227
01:56:52.040 --> 01:56:54.240
- But now it's your...
- Yes.

2228
01:56:54.320 --> 01:56:55.640
Let me look at you.

2229
01:56:56.920 --> 01:56:58.640
- God.
- What?

2230
01:56:58.760 --> 01:57:01.120
- Do I look different?
- Yes.

2231
01:57:01.240 --> 01:57:02.960
As different as you?

2232
01:57:03.080 --> 01:57:05.360
- You're thinner. Your eyes look...
- What?

2233
01:57:05.480 --> 01:57:07.880
- I can't believe you're here.
- Nor can I.

2234
01:57:07.960 --> 01:57:09.520
- I...
- Why didn't you let me see you?

2235
01:57:11.200 --> 01:57:13.160
You've been here almost a week.

2236
01:57:13.240 --> 01:57:15.440
I've tried every day,
three or four times.

2237
01:57:15.560 --> 01:57:16.680
You know that.

2238
01:57:16.800 --> 01:57:20.200
I was standing under your window
and you wouldn't even open the curtains.

2239
01:57:20.360 --> 01:57:22.760
- And you were standing there.
- I know.

2240
01:57:22.840 --> 01:57:25.120
- I saw you knew I was there.
- I know.

2241
01:57:26.320 --> 01:57:27.760
Made me feel like some desperate...

2242
01:57:28.960 --> 01:57:31.480
Made me feel mad... Made me...

2243
01:57:31.600 --> 01:57:33.480
Come here after everything
and then not see me, not...

2244
01:57:33.600 --> 01:57:35.760
- I thought you might hate me.
- Hate you?

2245
01:57:35.880 --> 01:57:37.680
Well, I don't want you to hate me.

2246
01:57:39.280 --> 01:57:43.760
Every night I've been having
these dreams where I see you,

2247
01:57:44.560 --> 01:57:45.960
here, by the lake,

2248
01:57:46.080 --> 01:57:50.200
but you don't know me
and you can't recognise me and...

2249
01:57:50.360 --> 01:57:53.840
I've walked here every day.
I have wanted...

2250
01:57:53.960 --> 01:57:55.240
I have...

2251
01:57:58.480 --> 01:58:00.640
- Let's sit down.
- Sit down?

2252
01:58:00.760 --> 01:58:04.840
Yes, let's sit down and talk.

2253
01:58:06.040 --> 01:58:09.400
Here, now, at last.

2254
01:58:11.800 --> 01:58:13.040
Come and sit down with me.

2255
01:58:14.960 --> 01:58:17.320
Come and sit down with me
in the warm.

2256
01:58:18.400 --> 01:58:20.440
In here in the warm.

2257
01:58:24.560 --> 01:58:26.000
(Gasps)

2258
01:58:26.120 --> 01:58:27.080
Don't cry.

2259
01:58:27.240 --> 01:58:29.360
I'm not, I'm not crying.

2260
01:58:33.600 --> 01:58:34.960
I actually...

2261
01:58:36.400 --> 01:58:38.600
I actually haven't cried
since I left here.

2262
01:58:39.640 --> 01:58:41.280
For... What is it?

2263
01:58:41.360 --> 01:58:44.560
What has it been? Five? Six?

2264
01:58:46.640 --> 01:58:48.760
Six years?

2265
01:58:50.200 --> 01:58:51.400
I've just...

2266
01:58:51.840 --> 01:58:53.680
...not cried in all that time.

2267
01:58:55.200 --> 01:58:57.520
And then, yesterday,

2268
01:58:57.600 --> 01:58:59.440
I came here

2269
01:59:00.400 --> 01:59:02.600
and I saw our stage,

2270
01:59:02.680 --> 01:59:04.800
still up and intact,

2271
01:59:04.880 --> 01:59:07.880
and it... just made me cry.

2272
01:59:09.120 --> 01:59:11.040
I am the seagull, remember?

2273
01:59:11.720 --> 01:59:13.280
No, that's not right. Sorry. It's not...

2274
01:59:13.440 --> 01:59:15.360
It's not right. Sorry.

2275
01:59:15.440 --> 01:59:16.440
Nina...

2276
01:59:16.600 --> 01:59:19.280
No, no, no, it's OK, cos...
cos I felt much better after crying.

2277
01:59:20.600 --> 01:59:22.240
And I'm not crying anymore.

2278
01:59:22.360 --> 01:59:23.320
See?

2279
01:59:25.440 --> 01:59:26.720
Nina...

2280
01:59:26.880 --> 01:59:28.160
You're a writer.

2281
01:59:30.440 --> 01:59:32.920
You are a writer.

2282
01:59:35.120 --> 01:59:37.360
And I am an actress.

2283
01:59:39.240 --> 01:59:40.240
How's that?

2284
01:59:42.520 --> 01:59:43.760
How did that happen?

2285
01:59:45.080 --> 01:59:48.000
From being here and...

2286
01:59:48.080 --> 01:59:49.920
and loving you

2287
01:59:50.800 --> 01:59:54.320
and dreaming of being famous.

2288
01:59:56.160 --> 01:59:57.800
And I was never gonna do it.

2289
01:59:59.320 --> 02:00:01.640
I was gonna stay here.

2290
02:00:01.720 --> 02:00:03.920
Always, forever.

2291
02:00:05.960 --> 02:00:08.360
But it was nice to dream,

2292
02:00:09.960 --> 02:00:11.960
and it was nice to talk.

2293
02:00:14.600 --> 02:00:17.200
And then we got caught
in something, I think.

2294
02:00:18.880 --> 02:00:20.280
And we can't get out.

2295
02:00:23.800 --> 02:00:25.280
I'm getting a coach tomorrow

2296
02:00:25.440 --> 02:00:28.160
and I'm going to be groped
for a whole winter.

2297
02:00:28.320 --> 02:00:30.160
- What? Why?
- I'm doing a play.

2298
02:00:30.320 --> 02:00:34.880
Cos... I should be acting, shouldn't I?

2299
02:00:37.720 --> 02:00:39.640
Because that's why
I did all of this, isn't it?

2300
02:00:42.160 --> 02:00:44.480
I'm doing a play. Well...

2301
02:00:44.640 --> 02:00:48.760
A play... yeah.
Yeah, you really don't want to know.

2302
02:00:52.240 --> 02:00:56.360
I should, I... I should... I should go.
I should... I should go. I...

2303
02:00:56.520 --> 02:00:58.040
Just, it's all...

2304
02:00:59.120 --> 02:01:00.480
It's all just...

2305
02:01:04.520 --> 02:01:07.520
Life's really rough, isn't it?

2306
02:01:09.120 --> 02:01:10.080
Nina.

2307
02:01:11.040 --> 02:01:12.400
It's a rough life.

2308
02:01:15.080 --> 02:01:20.360
If I thought my life was bad here before,
I really had no idea.

2309
02:01:24.560 --> 02:01:25.920
I hated you.

2310
02:01:28.040 --> 02:01:30.040
Every letter you sent me I tore up.

2311
02:01:31.200 --> 02:01:33.320
I'd burn them.

2312
02:01:33.480 --> 02:01:35.240
I burned photos of you.

2313
02:01:36.480 --> 02:01:38.800
I'd pray bad things for you.

2314
02:01:38.960 --> 02:01:41.760
I hated you so much.

2315
02:01:41.840 --> 02:01:45.400
I hated you... so much.

2316
02:01:46.360 --> 02:01:47.920
I understand. I...

2317
02:01:48.080 --> 02:01:52.320
But all it ever did was prove
that... I can't stop.

2318
02:01:54.800 --> 02:01:56.160
I can't stop loving you.

2319
02:01:58.440 --> 02:02:00.960
It doesn't matter
what passes between us.

2320
02:02:01.880 --> 02:02:02.840
I love you.

2321
02:02:04.800 --> 02:02:06.240
Ever since you left

2322
02:02:07.200 --> 02:02:08.640
it's been unbearable.

2323
02:02:09.960 --> 02:02:11.680
My life's been on fire without you.

2324
02:02:11.800 --> 02:02:15.440
- Why are you...?
- It hurts. It... hurts without you.

2325
02:02:16.720 --> 02:02:18.840
I feel old. I feel like...

2326
02:02:22.920 --> 02:02:24.720
I kiss the ground you walk on.

2327
02:02:26.680 --> 02:02:31.200
Everywhere I look I see your face,

2328
02:02:31.360 --> 02:02:32.640
the girl of my dreams.

2329
02:02:32.720 --> 02:02:35.120
Why does he say these things?
Why does he say them?

2330
02:02:35.240 --> 02:02:36.200
I love you.

2331
02:02:38.200 --> 02:02:39.280
Please stay.

2332
02:02:41.080 --> 02:02:42.040
Please.

2333
02:02:43.840 --> 02:02:45.240
I'd do anything.

2334
02:02:47.680 --> 02:02:50.320
I'm twenty-eight years old,
and knowing you,

2335
02:02:50.400 --> 02:02:52.120
loving you,

2336
02:02:52.240 --> 02:02:54.440
were the only years of my life
worth living.

2337
02:02:55.480 --> 02:02:56.920
Only time I've been happy.

2338
02:02:59.040 --> 02:03:01.160
Please don't take that all
away from me again.

2339
02:03:08.520 --> 02:03:10.120
I used to be able to write.

2340
02:03:11.680 --> 02:03:14.680
I was unhappy,
but I could do something,

2341
02:03:14.800 --> 02:03:15.960
make something.

2342
02:03:17.200 --> 02:03:18.800
I can't even do that anymore.

2343
02:03:20.360 --> 02:03:23.920
I'm in some prison and whatever I write,
it strengthens the bars,

2344
02:03:24.560 --> 02:03:27.760
cos it's all dead and stale.

2345
02:03:29.480 --> 02:03:30.640
Because I am.

2346
02:03:34.280 --> 02:03:35.240
Please stay.

2347
02:03:36.560 --> 02:03:37.520
Stay here.

2348
02:03:39.520 --> 02:03:40.920
Or let me come with you.

2349
02:03:42.640 --> 02:03:43.680
Anything.

2350
02:03:45.040 --> 02:03:46.160
Anything. Please.

2351
02:03:46.280 --> 02:03:47.440
Your mother is here.

2352
02:03:49.720 --> 02:03:51.840
My uncle's dying.

2353
02:03:52.000 --> 02:03:54.080
They sent for her.

2354
02:03:54.200 --> 02:03:56.640
You kiss the ground I walk on.

2355
02:03:56.720 --> 02:04:00.560
You kiss the ground I walk on. Why?

2356
02:04:01.720 --> 02:04:03.440
Why? You should kill me.

2357
02:04:03.560 --> 02:04:05.200
You should shoot me.
I need a rest.

2358
02:04:05.360 --> 02:04:06.520
You can rest.

2359
02:04:06.640 --> 02:04:09.400
I am so tired, Kostya.

2360
02:04:09.520 --> 02:04:11.520
I... I am the seagull.

2361
02:04:11.600 --> 02:04:16.240
No, no, no. That's not right.
I'm the actress, aren't I?

2362
02:04:16.800 --> 02:04:18.080
Oh, your mother!

2363
02:04:20.640 --> 02:04:22.360
Your mother is here.
Does that mean...?

2364
02:04:24.680 --> 02:04:27.560
Is he...? Is he here?

2365
02:04:27.640 --> 02:04:29.480
- I...
- It doesn't matter.

2366
02:04:29.640 --> 02:04:32.640
I can hear him. He is here.

2367
02:04:32.720 --> 02:04:34.080
It doesn't matter.

2368
02:04:39.920 --> 02:04:41.560
He never believed in me.

2369
02:04:44.240 --> 02:04:45.400
Which was...

2370
02:04:49.440 --> 02:04:54.640
Cos I only started to believe in myself
because I thought he did.

2371
02:04:59.200 --> 02:05:01.680
So I guess that means no one ever did.

2372
02:05:07.560 --> 02:05:09.440
He thought I was naive.

2373
02:05:11.600 --> 02:05:13.080
He was probably right.

2374
02:05:17.000 --> 02:05:19.400
But he laughed at my dreams.

2375
02:05:23.320 --> 02:05:25.640
And he laughed so hard

2376
02:05:27.800 --> 02:05:29.600
and for so long

2377
02:05:30.880 --> 02:05:33.360
that I laughed too.

2378
02:05:38.000 --> 02:05:39.920
And then there was everything else.

2379
02:05:43.000 --> 02:05:44.720
Money,

2380
02:05:44.840 --> 02:05:46.200
jealousy,

2381
02:05:50.680 --> 02:05:51.920
the baby.

2382
02:05:55.760 --> 02:05:56.720
(Sniffs)

2383
02:05:58.760 --> 02:05:59.800
The baby.

2384
02:06:05.640 --> 02:06:07.600
And then I couldn't act anymore.

2385
02:06:10.960 --> 02:06:13.760
I... I didn't know how to stand,

2386
02:06:16.040 --> 02:06:18.080
how to control my voice.

2387
02:06:20.440 --> 02:06:22.760
I forgot what you are meant
to do with your hands.

2388
02:06:23.880 --> 02:06:26.120
What to do with your hands on stage?

2389
02:06:26.200 --> 02:06:27.360
What do you do with them?

2390
02:06:31.880 --> 02:06:33.680
And then to stand there,

2391
02:06:36.680 --> 02:06:38.880
in front of everyone,

2392
02:06:42.520 --> 02:06:44.920
and know

2393
02:06:45.080 --> 02:06:49.760
that you are acting badly...

2394
02:06:56.160 --> 02:06:58.360
Everything you've ever wanted,

2395
02:07:02.400 --> 02:07:03.960
and you're bad at it.

2396
02:07:07.960 --> 02:07:09.320
And everyone knows it.

2397
02:07:10.760 --> 02:07:12.960
Everyone can see it.

2398
02:07:15.360 --> 02:07:16.720
You can yourself.

2399
02:07:16.800 --> 02:07:18.520
I saw you, I watched you.

2400
02:07:18.640 --> 02:07:21.600
I... I was the first one
to make you an actress.

2401
02:07:22.360 --> 02:07:23.800
I've seen you act.

2402
02:07:23.920 --> 02:07:26.600
- I saw... I saw you act.
- The seagull.

2403
02:07:26.680 --> 02:07:28.440
Not that that's me.

2404
02:07:28.600 --> 02:07:30.520
I'm the actress, aren't I?

2405
02:07:31.760 --> 02:07:33.520
An idea, Konstantin.

2406
02:07:33.600 --> 02:07:34.760
For a play.

2407
02:07:35.720 --> 02:07:37.240
For a... For a story.

2408
02:07:41.920 --> 02:07:46.720
A beautiful young seagull
loves the lake

2409
02:07:48.960 --> 02:07:50.400
and is happy...

2410
02:07:53.720 --> 02:07:55.000
...and free.

2411
02:07:59.600 --> 02:08:01.360
Then a man comes along

2412
02:08:04.720 --> 02:08:07.760
and, with nothing better to do,

2413
02:08:11.720 --> 02:08:13.160
destroys her.

2414
02:08:17.720 --> 02:08:21.320
You shot a seagull once, remember?
No, that's not right.

2415
02:08:21.400 --> 02:08:24.120
Sorry, that's not quite right.
I don't know why I said that.

2416
02:08:24.280 --> 02:08:26.680
I was telling you...
telling you... I was telling you...

2417
02:08:28.600 --> 02:08:30.520
...that it's different now.

2418
02:08:31.200 --> 02:08:32.280
It's different.

2419
02:08:35.320 --> 02:08:36.760
I love acting now.

2420
02:08:38.080 --> 02:08:39.320
It's different.

2421
02:08:44.600 --> 02:08:50.240
Everything I thought
it was about before, it isn't.

2422
02:08:51.840 --> 02:08:52.880
It wasn't.

2423
02:08:56.600 --> 02:08:59.240
It isn't about fame,

2424
02:08:59.320 --> 02:09:00.480
or money,

2425
02:09:01.160 --> 02:09:03.280
or... or...

2426
02:09:08.440 --> 02:09:10.160
It's about enduring.

2427
02:09:13.920 --> 02:09:15.840
Enduring it all.

2428
02:09:19.280 --> 02:09:23.320
It's a way you can endure it all.

2429
02:09:27.040 --> 02:09:29.360
And believe that you can.

2430
02:09:33.680 --> 02:09:35.920
- I haven't got that.
- What?

2431
02:09:37.840 --> 02:09:41.080
I don't have anything
to lead me through, because...

2432
02:09:43.080 --> 02:09:44.520
...I don't know where I'm going.

2433
02:09:47.320 --> 02:09:49.360
I just get images and dreams.

2434
02:09:50.880 --> 02:09:52.920
A cloud of fucking nightmares.

2435
02:09:57.280 --> 02:10:00.200
And I try to write it down,
but I don't know what it's all saying.

2436
02:10:01.920 --> 02:10:03.680
So, what purpose is it serving?

2437
02:10:05.280 --> 02:10:09.120
It's just nonsense
that's forced its way into my head.

2438
02:10:11.120 --> 02:10:12.280
So why am I doing it?

2439
02:10:13.640 --> 02:10:14.800
What's it for?

2440
02:10:19.200 --> 02:10:21.320
I don't believe
there's anything bigger guiding me.

2441
02:10:23.720 --> 02:10:25.080
And there's nothing in me.

2442
02:10:26.880 --> 02:10:28.040
I don't have a drive.

2443
02:10:29.000 --> 02:10:30.320
I don't have a calling.

2444
02:10:32.720 --> 02:10:34.200
I don't know what I should be doing.

2445
02:10:37.640 --> 02:10:39.080
What's gonna save me?

2446
02:10:41.680 --> 02:10:43.000
I thought it was gonna be you.

2447
02:10:43.160 --> 02:10:45.400
Shh, please, please, please, Kostya.
Don't anymore.

2448
02:10:46.240 --> 02:10:47.320
I have to go.

2449
02:10:48.960 --> 02:10:51.080
When I am a great actress,

2450
02:10:52.280 --> 02:10:54.080
you promise you'll come and see?

2451
02:10:55.880 --> 02:10:58.280
I'm so tired
I can hardly stand up anymore.

2452
02:10:58.920 --> 02:10:59.880
I...

2453
02:11:04.400 --> 02:11:05.560
I've just realised.

2454
02:11:07.880 --> 02:11:10.000
This is your uncle's house, not your...

2455
02:11:15.920 --> 02:11:18.040
She brought him with her.

2456
02:11:21.800 --> 02:11:23.720
You're gonna see them in a moment.
Please don't say anything.

2457
02:11:23.880 --> 02:11:25.040
I won't. I swear.

2458
02:11:29.360 --> 02:11:30.520
I still love him.

2459
02:11:33.280 --> 02:11:34.960
I love him more.

2460
02:11:35.800 --> 02:11:37.240
Idea for a play.

2461
02:11:39.840 --> 02:11:41.360
I love him desperately,

2462
02:11:43.080 --> 02:11:44.600
I love him passionately,

2463
02:11:46.720 --> 02:11:49.120
I love him to death,

2464
02:11:51.240 --> 02:11:52.400
to despair.

2465
02:12:00.440 --> 02:12:04.320
Do you remember
how simple it was before?

2466
02:12:10.080 --> 02:12:12.400
It was good, wasn't it?

2467
02:12:13.600 --> 02:12:14.560
Before.

2468
02:12:16.800 --> 02:12:17.760
Yes.

2469
02:12:19.360 --> 02:12:20.400
I remember that.

2470
02:12:22.480 --> 02:12:25.040
I remember your play.

2471
02:12:26.840 --> 02:12:27.960
Our play.

2472
02:12:29.000 --> 02:12:31.200
Do you? Do you remember?

2473
02:12:34.480 --> 02:12:36.480
Everything changes.

2474
02:12:39.800 --> 02:12:44.160
Everything but the soul.

2475
02:12:51.080 --> 02:12:53.760
It was all so much simpler before.

2476
02:13:00.200 --> 02:13:02.120
Come and see me when I'm good.

2477
02:13:07.560 --> 02:13:10.600
I hope my mother never finds out
that you were here...

2478
02:13:13.240 --> 02:13:14.200
...Nina.

2479
02:13:48.760 --> 02:13:51.440
(♪ Slow piano playing)

2480
02:14:00.400 --> 02:14:03.560
Now, OK?

2481
02:14:05.680 --> 02:14:06.720
This.

2482
02:14:07.680 --> 02:14:09.600
What on earth is that?

2483
02:14:09.680 --> 02:14:11.240
What does it look like?

2484
02:14:11.400 --> 02:14:14.680
- Can you put it away, please?
- No, I'm not coming back now.

2485
02:14:14.800 --> 02:14:16.640
It's not living here forever.

2486
02:14:17.920 --> 02:14:20.200
You saying you don't remember this?

2487
02:14:27.240 --> 02:14:28.200
No, no.

2488
02:14:29.720 --> 02:14:32.040
Honestly, I really have
no recollection whatsoever of this.

2489
02:14:32.160 --> 02:14:33.200
(Gunshot)

2490
02:14:33.320 --> 02:14:34.680
What was that?

2491
02:14:34.840 --> 02:14:37.520
No panic, no panic.

2492
02:14:38.880 --> 02:14:43.280
I suspect it's something of mine.
I left my bag out with the bike.

2493
02:14:43.400 --> 02:14:46.160
What the hell do you keep
in that bag of yours?

2494
02:14:46.280 --> 02:14:49.640
Ask me no questions
and I'll tell you no lies.

2495
02:14:49.720 --> 02:14:51.360
God, it scared me.

2496
02:14:52.520 --> 02:14:53.840
It reminded me of...

2497
02:14:56.120 --> 02:14:58.720
Is the doctor an arms dealer now?

2498
02:14:58.840 --> 02:15:01.520
Nothing would surprise me of that man.

2499
02:15:02.280 --> 02:15:05.560
- We could play again.
- Yes.

2500
02:15:05.680 --> 02:15:06.640
Who's playing?

2501
02:15:08.160 --> 02:15:09.200
Everyone?

2502
02:15:10.080 --> 02:15:11.800
Any confessions, Doctor?

2503
02:15:12.880 --> 02:15:14.880
In due course.

2504
02:15:14.960 --> 02:15:16.320
Due course.

2505
02:15:20.160 --> 02:15:22.640
So, what is the story with this bird?

2506
02:15:24.840 --> 02:15:26.080
This is yours?

2507
02:15:30.240 --> 02:15:33.800
Well, I'm not taking that home with me.
I can't for the life of me remember.

2508
02:15:33.960 --> 02:15:38.240
Extraordinary, quite extraordinary.

2509
02:15:41.200 --> 02:15:42.160
Get her out of here.

2510
02:15:46.000 --> 02:15:46.960
What?

2511
02:15:47.520 --> 02:15:51.160
However you like, but get her away
from that door and out this room.

2512
02:16:10.560 --> 02:16:11.600
Why?

2513
02:16:14.560 --> 02:16:15.840
Konstantin's...

2514
02:16:21.120 --> 02:16:23.160
The fact is, Konstantin's...

2515
02:16:29.560 --> 02:16:30.640
Yes.

2516
02:16:45.320 --> 02:16:48.000
(Audience applauds)
